1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,976
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:01,976 --> 00:00:03,944
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:03,944 --> 00:00:06,404
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:13,640
PHILLIP ISOLA: OK.

5
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:14,660
Welcome again, everyone.

6
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:15,660
Sorry for the delay.

7
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:17,080
So, yeah, we'll get started.

8
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:20,240
Today is going to be about transformers.

9
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:24,760
So hopefully you're all excited, because this is the architecture

10
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:25,940
that you should use today.

11
00:00:25,940 --> 00:00:27,310
Like, this will change.

12
00:00:27,310 --> 00:00:29,060
Next year, there'll be a new architecture.

13
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:30,440
Don't worry, things will change.

14
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:32,780
But this is the one that will get you the jobs today.

15
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:33,280
OK.

16
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:36,760
This is going to be a little bit more of a practical lecture.

17
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:40,640
With graph nets, we talked about the general class

18
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:44,008
of all the functions that could represent, and so forth.

19
00:00:44,008 --> 00:00:45,800
And there's so many different possible ways

20
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:47,825
of parameterizing graph nets.

21
00:00:47,825 --> 00:00:49,200
But with transformers, we're just

22
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,520
going to say there's one concrete instantiation that

23
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:53,100
works.

24
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:55,780
And that's what we'll learn about today.

25
00:00:55,780 --> 00:01:00,490
So we'll talk about three key ideas behind transformers.

26
00:01:00,490 --> 00:01:03,850
One is this idea of operating over tokens.

27
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:06,210
The next, this is going to be the only thing that's

28
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:06,893
actually new.

29
00:01:06,893 --> 00:01:08,310
It's going to be called attention.

30
00:01:08,310 --> 00:01:12,035
Everything else will actually be just new names for old ideas.

31
00:01:12,035 --> 00:01:13,410
And the last one we'll be talking

32
00:01:13,410 --> 00:01:15,952
more about, positional encoding, which we've seen a few times

33
00:01:15,952 --> 00:01:17,770
now.

34
00:01:17,770 --> 00:01:19,770
And then I'll show a few different architectures

35
00:01:19,770 --> 00:01:22,650
and some ways of using these things.

36
00:01:22,650 --> 00:01:25,470
And so, as I said, there's so many architectures,

37
00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:28,250
but right now there's just one to rule them all.

38
00:01:28,250 --> 00:01:30,450
This is the one-ring architecture.

39
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:30,950
Don't worry.

40
00:01:30,950 --> 00:01:31,673
It will change.

41
00:01:31,673 --> 00:01:33,590
This isn't going to be the final architecture,

42
00:01:33,590 --> 00:01:37,450
but this is the one right now.

43
00:01:37,450 --> 00:01:43,730
So there's this interesting book called Don Quixote,

44
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:46,370
written by Pierre Menard.

45
00:01:46,370 --> 00:01:49,970
And you probably are thinking I'm saying something wrong.

46
00:01:49,970 --> 00:01:52,930
It's written by Cervantes.

47
00:01:52,930 --> 00:01:57,370
But there's a short story that another guy, Borges,

48
00:01:57,370 --> 00:02:00,770
wrote about this guy, Pierre Menard, that

49
00:02:00,770 --> 00:02:04,730
rewrote the Don Quixote, word for word, exactly

50
00:02:04,730 --> 00:02:07,610
the same words, but with entirely new meaning.

51
00:02:07,610 --> 00:02:10,449
And in a short story, the meaning

52
00:02:10,449 --> 00:02:13,110
is new because the context is different.

53
00:02:13,110 --> 00:02:17,130
It's a different historical era, and the life of Pierre

54
00:02:17,130 --> 00:02:19,690
is different than the life of Cervantes.

55
00:02:19,690 --> 00:02:23,413
And you're going to hear the same exact story.

56
00:02:23,413 --> 00:02:25,830
And if you read this, you would read the same exact story,

57
00:02:25,830 --> 00:02:27,810
but it'd have entirely new meaning, because the context is

58
00:02:27,810 --> 00:02:28,570
different.

59
00:02:28,570 --> 00:02:30,328
And I think that's going to be a theme

60
00:02:30,328 --> 00:02:32,870
of the lecture today, and also the theme of this whole class.

61
00:02:32,870 --> 00:02:35,790
We'll keep on telling you the same things, maybe word for word

62
00:02:35,790 --> 00:02:36,830
the same things.

63
00:02:36,830 --> 00:02:39,090
But because you have new knowledge and new connections

64
00:02:39,090 --> 00:02:41,330
you can make, it'll have new meaning.

65
00:02:41,330 --> 00:02:45,342
So this is like a few pages, short story.

66
00:02:45,342 --> 00:02:47,050
If you want to read it, it's kind of fun.

67
00:02:47,050 --> 00:02:51,130
It's called Pierre Menard, author of The Quixote.

68
00:02:51,130 --> 00:02:57,580
So, everything old is new again.

69
00:02:57,580 --> 00:02:59,760
We're going to be riffing on the same themes.

70
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:02,240
So let's go back to convolutional architectures.

71
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:04,740
We're going to see a limitation of them.

72
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:07,540
So let's say that I want to ask, how many birds

73
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:08,295
are in this image.

74
00:03:08,295 --> 00:03:09,920
We saw that you can do that with a CNN.

75
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:12,560
You chop it up into pieces, you process with filters,

76
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:15,820
you aggregate the results, you count the birds.

77
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:17,860
But what if I want to say, is the top right

78
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:21,540
bird the same species as the bottom left bird?

79
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:23,100
So can a CNN do this?

80
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:25,700
Well, sure, if it's deep enough, if the receptive fields are

81
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:26,960
big enough, it can do it.

82
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:29,140
But it might not be the best way of doing that.

83
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:32,860
Because, remember, the CNN filters are all local.

84
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:35,740
And local filters are not going to be the most effective

85
00:03:35,740 --> 00:03:38,900
at making assessments about relationships that are more

86
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:41,380
global in nature or distant.

87
00:03:41,380 --> 00:03:44,120
So CNNs, they're built around this idea of locality.

88
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:46,460
They factorize the signal processing problem

89
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:51,020
into little local pieces that get put together, step by step.

90
00:03:51,020 --> 00:03:53,260
So they have the strong locality bias.

91
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:55,000
And that can be a good thing.

92
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:56,860
That's good for a lot of problems.

93
00:03:56,860 --> 00:03:58,460
If I want to understand something

94
00:03:58,460 --> 00:04:00,800
about the population in this room,

95
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:03,000
I don't need to go and look at Mars.

96
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,340
I can just locally look at what's going on in this room.

97
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:07,900
Most signals have-- the things that

98
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:10,120
matter to assessing them are all kind of local.

99
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:11,920
There's a smoothness to the world.

100
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:13,440
But that's not always a good idea.

101
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:16,060
And so transformers try to get around that.

102
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:19,140
Here's just a diagram looking at the receptive fields,

103
00:04:19,140 --> 00:04:21,820
essentially, of a simple CNN.

104
00:04:21,820 --> 00:04:24,740
So this is a two-layer convolutional network.

105
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:28,460
It's convolutional because every output neuron in this layer

106
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:32,300
is going to be taking input from just a local patch

107
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:34,460
of the first layer.

108
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:38,600
So it's like a length 3 filter over the input.

109
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:40,940
And if it's convolutional, the weights

110
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:43,500
would be shared among all these filters.

111
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:44,500
But notice what happens.

112
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:46,460
If I want to make some assessment

113
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:49,180
about the relationship between the first input

114
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:54,350
variable x1 and the seventh dimension of input X7.

115
00:04:54,350 --> 00:04:57,030
So on the second layer of the network,

116
00:04:57,030 --> 00:04:59,530
let's say this is the input, this is the first hidden layer,

117
00:04:59,530 --> 00:05:01,030
and this is the output.

118
00:05:01,030 --> 00:05:05,650
On this layer, only these hashed nodes,

119
00:05:05,650 --> 00:05:11,030
these hashed neurons have some dependency on x7.

120
00:05:11,030 --> 00:05:14,110
And for x1, it's these two over here.

121
00:05:14,110 --> 00:05:16,790
And on the next layer, the receptive field

122
00:05:16,790 --> 00:05:21,050
expands out by one, because the filter is a length 3 filter.

123
00:05:21,050 --> 00:05:23,410
And so you get a receptive field that expands by one.

124
00:05:23,410 --> 00:05:25,010
You can just trace the arrows back,

125
00:05:25,010 --> 00:05:30,990
see which neurons get some--

126
00:05:30,990 --> 00:05:33,690
imagine some dye is dropped onto that neuron,

127
00:05:33,690 --> 00:05:36,330
and the dye bleeds out over the edges of this graph.

128
00:05:36,330 --> 00:05:37,850
Which ones is it going to hit?

129
00:05:37,850 --> 00:05:40,150
Which are in the support of this neuron here?

130
00:05:40,150 --> 00:05:44,250
So it's just x1 is in the support of this neuron,

131
00:05:44,250 --> 00:05:45,610
this neuron and that neuron.

132
00:05:45,610 --> 00:05:48,850
And x7 is the support of the three neurons on the right.

133
00:05:48,850 --> 00:05:52,550
But if that's my output layer and I only have this depth 2

134
00:05:52,550 --> 00:05:55,390
convolutional network, then no neuron in the output

135
00:05:55,390 --> 00:05:59,010
has gotten dye from both x1 and x7.

136
00:05:59,010 --> 00:06:00,790
So it's impossible for this architecture

137
00:06:00,790 --> 00:06:02,710
to have made a decision that depends

138
00:06:02,710 --> 00:06:07,950
on the joint configuration of x1 and x7.

139
00:06:07,950 --> 00:06:09,490
So I can always make a deeper CNN,

140
00:06:09,490 --> 00:06:11,430
but this dye just bleeds out slowly

141
00:06:11,430 --> 00:06:12,850
in convolutional networks.

142
00:06:15,570 --> 00:06:19,210
So the idea of attention is to say, well,

143
00:06:19,210 --> 00:06:24,590
let's have an operation that allows us to look more globally.

144
00:06:24,590 --> 00:06:27,043
But the problem, there's another way of doing this.

145
00:06:27,043 --> 00:06:28,710
What is another way that we saw that you

146
00:06:28,710 --> 00:06:33,412
can have neural networks that process data globally?

147
00:06:33,412 --> 00:06:34,870
What is one of the architectures we

148
00:06:34,870 --> 00:06:38,690
saw that doesn't have this locality bias of CNNs?

149
00:06:38,690 --> 00:06:40,250
Anyone want to shout out?

150
00:06:40,250 --> 00:06:41,430
AUDIENCE: Fully connected.

151
00:06:41,430 --> 00:06:44,310
PHILLIP ISOLA: Fully connected network, just a vanilla MLP.

152
00:06:44,310 --> 00:06:48,160
And a vanilla MLP with fully connected layers,

153
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:51,500
every single output neuron is connected to every input neuron.

154
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:55,180
So information is aggregated immediately globally.

155
00:06:55,180 --> 00:06:57,760
But CNN said no, no, we want to chop it up and factorize

156
00:06:57,760 --> 00:06:58,600
the problem.

157
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:01,220
And then attention says, OK, no, we want to look globally,

158
00:07:01,220 --> 00:07:04,333
but we don't want to have every neuron connected

159
00:07:04,333 --> 00:07:06,000
to every neuron, because that would just

160
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:07,260
be too much processing.

161
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:11,300
That would mean that for a mapping from Rn to Rn,

162
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:14,260
we'd have n squared edges and n squared parameters.

163
00:07:14,260 --> 00:07:17,400
And it would just be too compute heavy, too many parameters,

164
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:19,520
too much to learn.

165
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:21,840
Attention says, well, we'll look globally,

166
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:23,500
but we'll look sparsely globally.

167
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:25,520
So when you get a new question, you'll

168
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:28,298
just attend to the parts of the input signal that matter.

169
00:07:28,298 --> 00:07:29,840
So if I want to count how many birds,

170
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:32,080
I'll attend to these locations.

171
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:34,720
It's exactly coming from the idea

172
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:38,665
of attention in the human brain or in your own life experience.

173
00:07:38,665 --> 00:07:40,040
You can attend to the things that

174
00:07:40,040 --> 00:07:41,382
are relevant to your problem.

175
00:07:41,382 --> 00:07:43,840
If your problem changes, you'll attend to different things.

176
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:46,620
So you'll attend to these two birds

177
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:48,960
if the question is the top right bird the same

178
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:50,300
as the bottom left bird.

179
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:52,508
And if the question is, what is the color of the sky,

180
00:07:52,508 --> 00:07:56,280
you'll just attend to the sky region of the image.

181
00:07:56,280 --> 00:07:58,260
So that's the intuitive idea of attention.

182
00:07:58,260 --> 00:08:00,280
It's meant to model what humans might

183
00:08:00,280 --> 00:08:03,240
be doing when they process images

184
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:05,847
or other types of signals.

185
00:08:05,847 --> 00:08:07,680
And we're going to talk about mathematically

186
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:09,200
how that can be done.

187
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:13,320
So I'm going to go through these three architectural pieces

188
00:08:13,320 --> 00:08:14,645
of transformers.

189
00:08:14,645 --> 00:08:16,020
Attention will be the second one.

190
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:17,860
That's really the new one in transformers.

191
00:08:17,860 --> 00:08:19,520
And then tokens and positional encodes

192
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:22,280
will be just the same as you've seen before.

193
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:27,040
So new idea-- not really new, but sort of new idea-- number 1

194
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:28,120
is tokens.

195
00:08:28,120 --> 00:08:32,400
So it's a new name for what we actually saw in the GNN lecture.

196
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:34,480
So a token is a vector of neurons.

197
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:39,440
This is how I'll define it for the purpose of our course.

198
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:43,520
So in the GNN lecture, we talked about node attribute vectors

199
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:44,970
or node feature vectors.

200
00:08:44,970 --> 00:08:47,410
So every node has a vector associated with it.

201
00:08:47,410 --> 00:08:49,850
And we're going to give that just a new name, which

202
00:08:49,850 --> 00:08:51,050
is a token.

203
00:08:51,050 --> 00:08:53,210
So a token is a vector of neurons.

204
00:08:53,210 --> 00:08:55,090
And the way to think of it is that it's

205
00:08:55,090 --> 00:08:57,730
like a little encapsulated factor

206
00:08:57,730 --> 00:08:59,410
in my computational graph.

207
00:08:59,410 --> 00:09:02,570
It's going to be a little packet of information that

208
00:09:02,570 --> 00:09:08,930
will be a little bundle of information that will all be

209
00:09:08,930 --> 00:09:10,850
processed in a modular fashion.

210
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:12,270
So we'll have a set of tokens.

211
00:09:12,270 --> 00:09:13,850
And these are the units that we're

212
00:09:13,850 --> 00:09:16,130
operating over in transformers.

213
00:09:16,130 --> 00:09:18,330
So in graph nets, the tokens were the nodes

214
00:09:18,330 --> 00:09:20,970
of the graph or the feature vectors associated

215
00:09:20,970 --> 00:09:23,450
with the nodes.

216
00:09:23,450 --> 00:09:26,390
So we can have an array of neurons,

217
00:09:26,390 --> 00:09:30,330
and that's what we operate over in MLPs and vanilla

218
00:09:30,330 --> 00:09:32,090
neural networks.

219
00:09:32,090 --> 00:09:33,970
But in transformers, we should always

220
00:09:33,970 --> 00:09:38,050
be thinking about the basic data structure as an array of tokens,

221
00:09:38,050 --> 00:09:39,730
as opposed to an array of neurons.

222
00:09:39,730 --> 00:09:41,290
So it's an array of vectors or array

223
00:09:41,290 --> 00:09:43,855
of little encapsulated bits of information.

224
00:09:43,855 --> 00:09:45,730
You could also generalize the notion of token

225
00:09:45,730 --> 00:09:48,010
to be not a vector, but it's some array

226
00:09:48,010 --> 00:09:49,488
of structured information.

227
00:09:49,488 --> 00:09:52,030
In transformers, the structured information is just a vector,

228
00:09:52,030 --> 00:09:53,780
but there might be generalizations to this

229
00:09:53,780 --> 00:09:58,190
that come about later in 2025 for the next year.

230
00:09:58,190 --> 00:10:02,130
So I do want to note that I'm using the word "token"

231
00:10:02,130 --> 00:10:04,467
in a way that is not completely standard.

232
00:10:04,467 --> 00:10:07,050
I'm using it in a way that shows up in various computer vision

233
00:10:07,050 --> 00:10:10,650
literature, but is a little bit less common in natural language

234
00:10:10,650 --> 00:10:11,270
processing.

235
00:10:11,270 --> 00:10:13,170
So in natural language processing,

236
00:10:13,170 --> 00:10:15,810
people mostly talk about tokens as being

237
00:10:15,810 --> 00:10:18,390
the discrete units of the vocabulary that we're modeling.

238
00:10:18,390 --> 00:10:20,850
So it's like a token is the letter

239
00:10:20,850 --> 00:10:25,015
A, B, C. There's 26 tokens for the English alphabet.

240
00:10:25,015 --> 00:10:27,390
Or we could tokenize with a different kind of vocabulary.

241
00:10:27,390 --> 00:10:30,270
It could be that every English word is a separate token.

242
00:10:30,270 --> 00:10:33,050
So there's discrete units of the vocabulary that I'm operating

243
00:10:33,050 --> 00:10:35,730
over is what natural language people usually

244
00:10:35,730 --> 00:10:36,550
mean by the tokens.

245
00:10:36,550 --> 00:10:40,220
But it's a kind of weird definition.

246
00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:44,380
I prefer to think of the tokens as the chunks of information

247
00:10:44,380 --> 00:10:46,550
that you are factorizing your problem into.

248
00:10:46,550 --> 00:10:48,300
It's a more general definition, and that's

249
00:10:48,300 --> 00:10:50,258
the one we're going to go with in this lecture.

250
00:10:50,258 --> 00:10:52,500
So token is a vector of neurons.

251
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:57,140
And a set of tokens is how I will represent some data.

252
00:10:57,140 --> 00:11:01,137
So just like I could have ConvNets

253
00:11:01,137 --> 00:11:02,720
that operate over an array of neurons,

254
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:05,420
I could have convolution over tokens that operates

255
00:11:05,420 --> 00:11:06,780
over an array of tokens.

256
00:11:06,780 --> 00:11:09,275
That would just be like multi-channel convolution.

257
00:11:09,275 --> 00:11:10,900
So I can have different data structures

258
00:11:10,900 --> 00:11:12,567
built on top of tokens, where now tokens

259
00:11:12,567 --> 00:11:14,580
are the new units, the new encapsulated units

260
00:11:14,580 --> 00:11:17,120
of atomic information.

261
00:11:17,120 --> 00:11:20,040
In a neural net, we have scalar valued neurons.

262
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:23,460
In a token neural net, we have vector valued units.

263
00:11:26,420 --> 00:11:30,695
And we could have a set that's unstructured, unordered.

264
00:11:30,695 --> 00:11:32,320
And we could also have a set of tokens.

265
00:11:32,320 --> 00:11:35,500
And in transformer land, the main thing we operate over

266
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:37,400
is actually sets, as opposed to arrays.

267
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:41,840
But there's always variations on this theme that you can make up.

268
00:11:41,840 --> 00:11:44,940
So let's use an image processing example.

269
00:11:44,940 --> 00:11:48,620
But I want to just make sure we keep in mind

270
00:11:48,620 --> 00:11:52,220
that transformers, maybe why they're so popular

271
00:11:52,220 --> 00:11:56,460
is because they're very agnostic to the domain.

272
00:11:56,460 --> 00:12:00,000
The recipe is once you turn your data into a set of tokens,

273
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,407
then you just use a transformer.

274
00:12:01,407 --> 00:12:03,740
And this is one of the reasons they just dominate across

275
00:12:03,740 --> 00:12:04,840
so many different domains.

276
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:08,177
And basically, all domains where deep learning is applied

277
00:12:08,177 --> 00:12:10,260
that I know of, I think transformers are currently

278
00:12:10,260 --> 00:12:13,340
the most popular way of doing it,

279
00:12:13,340 --> 00:12:16,820
or at least the most performant way of doing it.

280
00:12:16,820 --> 00:12:19,580
So that's one of the powers is they're very

281
00:12:19,580 --> 00:12:22,140
agnostic to the input signal.

282
00:12:22,140 --> 00:12:25,160
Their previous methods, like CNNs,

283
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:27,300
were much more tied to the locality properties

284
00:12:27,300 --> 00:12:28,500
of the signal.

285
00:12:28,500 --> 00:12:31,542
So how do you turn your data into tokens?

286
00:12:31,542 --> 00:12:33,000
And then we'll just process tokens.

287
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,160
That's the general idea of transformers.

288
00:12:35,160 --> 00:12:37,750
First, the domain expert turns data into tokens,

289
00:12:37,750 --> 00:12:42,150
and then generic transformer processes tokens.

290
00:12:42,150 --> 00:12:44,428
So for images, this is the standard way of doing it.

291
00:12:44,428 --> 00:12:46,970
But you can imagine a lot of other ways that one could do it.

292
00:12:46,970 --> 00:12:49,190
But you just break up the image into patches.

293
00:12:49,190 --> 00:12:51,470
Just like a ConvNet, you chop it up into patches,

294
00:12:51,470 --> 00:12:54,270
and then you turn each patch into a token

295
00:12:54,270 --> 00:12:57,310
by just projecting it into a flat vector.

296
00:12:57,310 --> 00:12:59,630
And you could use some matrix that you

297
00:12:59,630 --> 00:13:03,070
multiply the pixel values by W tokenize

298
00:13:03,070 --> 00:13:04,592
to get a D dimensional vector.

299
00:13:04,592 --> 00:13:06,050
And that could be a learned matrix,

300
00:13:06,050 --> 00:13:08,030
or it could be somehow hard-coded.

301
00:13:08,030 --> 00:13:11,750
So just a linear operation.

302
00:13:11,750 --> 00:13:14,850
And I've basically already said this--

303
00:13:14,850 --> 00:13:16,590
but when operating over pixels, we're

304
00:13:16,590 --> 00:13:19,950
operating over scalar-valued measurements.

305
00:13:19,950 --> 00:13:21,830
What's the red color at that pixel?

306
00:13:21,830 --> 00:13:23,230
When operating over tokens, we're

307
00:13:23,230 --> 00:13:26,310
representing the input as an array

308
00:13:26,310 --> 00:13:27,770
of vector-valued measurements.

309
00:13:27,770 --> 00:13:30,630
And the vectors are just the projection

310
00:13:30,630 --> 00:13:33,590
of a set of RGB measurements in a patch

311
00:13:33,590 --> 00:13:39,110
onto a fixed dimensional vector in Rd.

312
00:13:39,110 --> 00:13:39,730
Question?

313
00:13:39,730 --> 00:13:42,310
AUDIENCE: All the tokens are generally the same size?

314
00:13:42,310 --> 00:13:43,790
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, they do.

315
00:13:43,790 --> 00:13:46,630
So do all tokens generally have the same size?

316
00:13:46,630 --> 00:13:47,570
Yeah, usually they do.

317
00:13:47,570 --> 00:13:52,630
They're generically like a set of vectors in Rd.

318
00:13:52,630 --> 00:13:56,480
And that is an interesting thing to maybe do

319
00:13:56,480 --> 00:13:58,230
for a final project or a research project.

320
00:13:58,230 --> 00:14:01,350
I think variable size tokens could be really interesting.

321
00:14:01,350 --> 00:14:03,910
It's not a standard way of doing it.

322
00:14:03,910 --> 00:14:06,550
Probably there are some challenges with hardware.

323
00:14:06,550 --> 00:14:09,170
Another idea of transformers is that they just match really,

324
00:14:09,170 --> 00:14:13,148
really well to the GPU hardware and the software that we use.

325
00:14:13,148 --> 00:14:14,690
And if you have variable-size tokens,

326
00:14:14,690 --> 00:14:16,690
now there's something you have to keep track of.

327
00:14:16,690 --> 00:14:20,730
And how do how do you map that onto your hardware?

328
00:14:20,730 --> 00:14:21,790
It might be non-trivial.

329
00:14:21,790 --> 00:14:23,470
Question in the very back.

330
00:14:23,470 --> 00:14:26,725
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

331
00:14:27,960 --> 00:14:29,210
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, question.

332
00:14:29,210 --> 00:14:30,650
If you're tokenizing an image, do you

333
00:14:30,650 --> 00:14:31,890
want your patches to overlap?

334
00:14:31,890 --> 00:14:34,300
And the answer is in standard architectures,

335
00:14:34,300 --> 00:14:36,730
no, you generally don't overlap.

336
00:14:36,730 --> 00:14:37,980
But that might be a good idea.

337
00:14:37,980 --> 00:14:39,300
Just like in convolutional networks,

338
00:14:39,300 --> 00:14:40,700
you can set the stride that's a parameter.

339
00:14:40,700 --> 00:14:42,500
And you can have that same parameter here.

340
00:14:42,500 --> 00:14:44,640
But the current standard way of doing it

341
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:46,740
is no overlap, as far as I know.

342
00:14:46,740 --> 00:14:47,708
That might change.

343
00:14:47,708 --> 00:14:49,500
We'll hear one more, and then I'll move on.

344
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:51,048
AUDIENCE: So are the tokens just,

345
00:14:51,048 --> 00:14:52,840
like when you take the patches, the patches

346
00:14:52,840 --> 00:14:53,967
are also just pixels.

347
00:14:53,967 --> 00:14:54,800
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

348
00:14:54,800 --> 00:14:56,758
AUDIENCE: And it would just be the tokens would

349
00:14:56,758 --> 00:14:59,320
be the pixels in vector form.

350
00:14:59,320 --> 00:15:01,640
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

351
00:15:01,640 --> 00:15:03,640
The question is, are the patches just pixels.

352
00:15:03,640 --> 00:15:04,400
Yes.

353
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:07,340
So you flatten the patch into just a list of numbers.

354
00:15:07,340 --> 00:15:10,160
It's a long vector that is the length, depending

355
00:15:10,160 --> 00:15:11,400
on the size of the patch.

356
00:15:11,400 --> 00:15:14,080
And then you project that into a whatever dimensionality

357
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:18,120
you want to process D dimensions.

358
00:15:18,120 --> 00:15:22,000
So you can tokenize anything.

359
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,320
And again, this is the power.

360
00:15:24,320 --> 00:15:27,165
The general strategy is you take your signal, your data you're

361
00:15:27,165 --> 00:15:29,040
processing, you chop it up into little chunks

362
00:15:29,040 --> 00:15:33,582
where the chunks make sense as the units of your domain.

363
00:15:33,582 --> 00:15:35,040
So usually that means local chunks,

364
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:38,320
because locality is a very good inductive bias.

365
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:40,520
So in images, the standard thing is chop up

366
00:15:40,520 --> 00:15:42,480
into non-overlapping patches.

367
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:44,040
In language, the standard thing is

368
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:46,840
to chop up into little snippets of words

369
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:48,520
which are called byte pairs.

370
00:15:48,520 --> 00:15:53,040
So I won't go into the details, but basically

371
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:55,360
the kind of phonemes or the units of the language

372
00:15:55,360 --> 00:15:57,680
that you're operating it over, you

373
00:15:57,680 --> 00:16:01,920
could operate over single characters or three characters

374
00:16:01,920 --> 00:16:04,200
or entire words, but the one that people like

375
00:16:04,200 --> 00:16:05,620
is just called a byte pair.

376
00:16:05,620 --> 00:16:10,160
It's like two byte representations of the language.

377
00:16:10,160 --> 00:16:12,180
So you can learn this for English.

378
00:16:12,180 --> 00:16:14,380
And there might be a byte pair that represents T-H,

379
00:16:14,380 --> 00:16:18,480
and there might be a byte pair that represents I-N-G. So

380
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:22,340
that's how you carve up English and other natural language.

381
00:16:22,340 --> 00:16:24,440
And how you carve up sounds will often just

382
00:16:24,440 --> 00:16:26,980
be chop into chunks like you do for an image.

383
00:16:30,530 --> 00:16:33,290
So once you have done that, then everything else

384
00:16:33,290 --> 00:16:34,430
is domain agnostic.

385
00:16:34,430 --> 00:16:36,930
So this is the only part where you have to actually consider

386
00:16:36,930 --> 00:16:37,870
your input domain.

387
00:16:37,870 --> 00:16:39,822
I mean, yes, when you think about the loss

388
00:16:39,822 --> 00:16:41,530
function and other formulations, yes, you

389
00:16:41,530 --> 00:16:42,780
think about your input domain.

390
00:16:42,780 --> 00:16:45,330
But this is the main part.

391
00:16:45,330 --> 00:16:48,670
So here's a notation that we're going to use.

392
00:16:48,670 --> 00:16:52,210
So we're going to think of the tokens as just vectors.

393
00:16:52,210 --> 00:16:55,290
And I'm often going to describe an array of tokens

394
00:16:55,290 --> 00:16:57,667
or a set of tokens, because it will turn out

395
00:16:57,667 --> 00:16:59,750
that actually the ordering is going to not matter.

396
00:16:59,750 --> 00:17:02,690
We'll have architectures that are permutation invariant

397
00:17:02,690 --> 00:17:06,310
over the ordering of the rows of that matrix as this matrix T.

398
00:17:06,310 --> 00:17:08,589
So I'm going to just transpose my token vectors.

399
00:17:08,589 --> 00:17:10,089
Now they're going to be row vectors.

400
00:17:10,089 --> 00:17:11,869
I'm going to stack them up.

401
00:17:11,869 --> 00:17:14,890
And that will be this matrix T that will

402
00:17:14,890 --> 00:17:16,349
simplify a lot of the math.

403
00:17:16,349 --> 00:17:17,849
So when we're operating over tokens,

404
00:17:17,849 --> 00:17:19,390
this is the notation we'll use.

405
00:17:19,390 --> 00:17:22,401
We have n tokens in the rows and d channels.

406
00:17:22,401 --> 00:17:24,609
So we're going to use the same dimensionality for all

407
00:17:24,609 --> 00:17:26,490
tokens to answer that question.

408
00:17:26,490 --> 00:17:29,167
And if you've tried to use different dimensionality,

409
00:17:29,167 --> 00:17:30,750
the notation will become more complex.

410
00:17:30,750 --> 00:17:34,870
And you'd have to do some more bookkeeping.

411
00:17:34,870 --> 00:17:39,210
And yeah, it would be harder.

412
00:17:39,210 --> 00:17:44,170
So just like with regular old MLPs

413
00:17:44,170 --> 00:17:47,350
in networks built out of tokens, we have two basic operations.

414
00:17:47,350 --> 00:17:49,270
One is a linear combination operation.

415
00:17:49,270 --> 00:17:53,270
And the other is a unit-wise nonlinearity.

416
00:17:53,270 --> 00:17:55,310
So what is a linear combination operation?

417
00:17:55,310 --> 00:17:58,210
It's just a generalization of the linear combination

418
00:17:58,210 --> 00:17:59,290
over neurons.

419
00:17:59,290 --> 00:18:01,310
So here's a linear combination over neurons.

420
00:18:01,310 --> 00:18:02,650
This is a linear layer.

421
00:18:02,650 --> 00:18:06,170
I have x in is a set of scalars-- x1, x2, x3.

422
00:18:06,170 --> 00:18:09,330
And x out is just another scalar.

423
00:18:09,330 --> 00:18:12,770
And I have a weighted combination, w1 times x1,

424
00:18:12,770 --> 00:18:13,890
and so forth.

425
00:18:13,890 --> 00:18:19,990
This is the regular old linear layer going from R3 to R1.

426
00:18:19,990 --> 00:18:22,733
So what is a linear combination of tokens look like?

427
00:18:22,733 --> 00:18:24,900
Oh, and right, we just can write that in matrix form

428
00:18:24,900 --> 00:18:26,140
as w times x in.

429
00:18:26,140 --> 00:18:29,917
You've done that a lot on the P sets and in other places.

430
00:18:29,917 --> 00:18:31,500
A linear combination of tokens is just

431
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:34,300
going to be a weighted combination of vectors.

432
00:18:34,300 --> 00:18:37,380
So I'll have vectors with D dimensions.

433
00:18:37,380 --> 00:18:40,363
But if I only have three tokens, three such vectors,

434
00:18:40,363 --> 00:18:41,780
I'll have three weights that I can

435
00:18:41,780 --> 00:18:44,380
learn or decide to set the values of to create

436
00:18:44,380 --> 00:18:45,860
a linear combination.

437
00:18:45,860 --> 00:18:50,977
So it's going to be a weight times the first vector,

438
00:18:50,977 --> 00:18:52,560
plus a weight times the second vector,

439
00:18:52,560 --> 00:18:55,860
plus a weight times the third vector.

440
00:18:55,860 --> 00:19:00,460
So you can represent that, again, in matrix notation,

441
00:19:00,460 --> 00:19:07,380
if we're using our T where T is n rows by d columns, where

442
00:19:07,380 --> 00:19:09,700
d is the dimensionality of each token vector.

443
00:19:09,700 --> 00:19:12,260
Then w times that matrix T is just

444
00:19:12,260 --> 00:19:16,160
like taking a linear combination of the rows.

445
00:19:16,160 --> 00:19:20,260
So you can already see here that the token linear combination

446
00:19:20,260 --> 00:19:23,140
is less expressive than if I did the same thing

447
00:19:23,140 --> 00:19:26,060
over all the individual neurons that make up those tokens.

448
00:19:26,060 --> 00:19:29,100
It's like a low rank transformation over the neurons,

449
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:31,660
because I only have three weights that

450
00:19:31,660 --> 00:19:33,500
determine that combination, but I actually

451
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:36,820
have 12 neurons that make up all of those tokens.

452
00:19:36,820 --> 00:19:38,620
And so you normally would have 12 weights

453
00:19:38,620 --> 00:19:43,300
if it were just a linear combination of neurons.

454
00:19:43,300 --> 00:19:48,000
So this is a less expressive family of layers.

455
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:52,900
In fact, it's a low rank operation.

456
00:19:52,900 --> 00:19:56,080
But we're thinking of the tokens as these bundles,

457
00:19:56,080 --> 00:19:58,400
these encapsulated units of operations.

458
00:19:58,400 --> 00:20:00,860
We've just now defined how to take linear combinations

459
00:20:00,860 --> 00:20:04,668
of those encapsulated units.

460
00:20:04,668 --> 00:20:06,460
So the next thing we need is the equivalent

461
00:20:06,460 --> 00:20:09,100
of a ReLU, the point-wise nonlinearity.

462
00:20:09,100 --> 00:20:11,960
And with tokens, we can have a token-wise nonlinearity.

463
00:20:11,960 --> 00:20:13,800
Instead of being a neuron-wise nonlinearity,

464
00:20:13,800 --> 00:20:15,900
it will be a nonlinearity that operates

465
00:20:15,900 --> 00:20:19,220
on each token independently and identically.

466
00:20:19,220 --> 00:20:23,510
So here's my point-wise nonlinearity from MLPs.

467
00:20:23,510 --> 00:20:26,730
We have x out is equal to, for each dimension of x in,

468
00:20:26,730 --> 00:20:28,890
we just take a ReLU of that dimension.

469
00:20:31,470 --> 00:20:33,390
And a token-wise nonlinearity is just

470
00:20:33,390 --> 00:20:36,350
going to be, for each token in my array of tokens,

471
00:20:36,350 --> 00:20:38,750
I will pass it through a nonlinear function F.

472
00:20:38,750 --> 00:20:41,110
And it will be the same nonlinear function applied

473
00:20:41,110 --> 00:20:44,950
to every single token.

474
00:20:44,950 --> 00:20:50,530
So typically in transformers, F is going to be itself an MLP.

475
00:20:50,530 --> 00:20:52,450
So transformers are like a meta architecture.

476
00:20:52,450 --> 00:20:53,910
Like, the units of the architecture

477
00:20:53,910 --> 00:20:57,950
are other neural networks.

478
00:20:57,950 --> 00:21:00,750
And you can also think of this operation as equivalent

479
00:21:00,750 --> 00:21:03,910
to a convolutional filter that is run

480
00:21:03,910 --> 00:21:05,990
across the sequence of tokens.

481
00:21:05,990 --> 00:21:09,403
It's a 1 by 1 convolutional filter.

482
00:21:09,403 --> 00:21:10,570
So what does that look like?

483
00:21:10,570 --> 00:21:11,650
It looks like this.

484
00:21:11,650 --> 00:21:13,350
I have a sequence of tokens.

485
00:21:13,350 --> 00:21:16,830
Now think of this as like a spatial sequence

486
00:21:16,830 --> 00:21:17,810
or temporal sequence.

487
00:21:17,810 --> 00:21:19,770
And the channels are in this token dimension.

488
00:21:19,770 --> 00:21:23,847
The vector dimensions of the token.

489
00:21:23,847 --> 00:21:25,430
And the convolutional filter will just

490
00:21:25,430 --> 00:21:27,990
be something that operates on each token independently

491
00:21:27,990 --> 00:21:29,010
and identically.

492
00:21:29,010 --> 00:21:32,590
So this is what the token-wise nonlinearity looks like.

493
00:21:32,590 --> 00:21:34,550
And you can also now map that in your brain

494
00:21:34,550 --> 00:21:37,850
onto a point-wise nonlinearity in an MLP,

495
00:21:37,850 --> 00:21:40,590
that would just be like I have vectors of length 1.

496
00:21:40,590 --> 00:21:43,390
And my operation them sliding across that sequence

497
00:21:43,390 --> 00:21:47,730
is a ReLU or a tanh or sigmoid, some point-wise nonlinearity.

498
00:21:51,350 --> 00:21:55,030
So now we've basically built what I'll call token nets.

499
00:21:55,030 --> 00:21:56,930
Another name for this would be graph nets.

500
00:21:56,930 --> 00:22:00,750
But I'm going to call it token nets for now, which is almost

501
00:22:00,750 --> 00:22:03,550
identical looking to a MLP.

502
00:22:03,550 --> 00:22:06,610
So MLP is linear combination of scalar-valued neurons,

503
00:22:06,610 --> 00:22:09,030
point-wise nonlinearity, and so on.

504
00:22:09,030 --> 00:22:12,390
And token net is linear combination of tokens.

505
00:22:12,390 --> 00:22:15,350
Take a weighted sum of these three items, followed

506
00:22:15,350 --> 00:22:18,800
by token-wise nonlinearity, so each arrow on the second row

507
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:22,560
is itself parameterized by an MLP-- it's

508
00:22:22,560 --> 00:22:24,100
a complicated nonlinear function--

509
00:22:24,100 --> 00:22:26,480
and then repeat that process.

510
00:22:26,480 --> 00:22:28,500
So the motif is the same.

511
00:22:28,500 --> 00:22:31,080
It's just over these more complicated units.

512
00:22:31,080 --> 00:22:33,105
As opposed to being scalar-valued units, which

513
00:22:33,105 --> 00:22:34,980
are called neurons, it's vector-valued units,

514
00:22:34,980 --> 00:22:38,560
which I'm calling tokens.

515
00:22:38,560 --> 00:22:41,840
So I already mentioned, but this is also

516
00:22:41,840 --> 00:22:44,520
equivalent to that unrolled GNN that I talked

517
00:22:44,520 --> 00:22:46,360
about a few lectures ago.

518
00:22:46,360 --> 00:22:51,420
So an unrolled GNN, we have this aggregate function,

519
00:22:51,420 --> 00:22:53,940
which is doing some kind of weighted combination.

520
00:22:53,940 --> 00:22:55,560
And then we have a combined function,

521
00:22:55,560 --> 00:23:00,500
which now you can understand to be a unit-wise,

522
00:23:00,500 --> 00:23:02,180
a node-wise nonlinearity.

523
00:23:02,180 --> 00:23:03,700
And then we repeat that process.

524
00:23:03,700 --> 00:23:06,000
And GNN is also often have the property that you

525
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,100
share weights across depth.

526
00:23:08,100 --> 00:23:09,760
You don't have to, but you can.

527
00:23:09,760 --> 00:23:11,360
Whereas transformers typically have

528
00:23:11,360 --> 00:23:13,652
the property that you don't share weights across depth,

529
00:23:13,652 --> 00:23:14,320
but you can.

530
00:23:14,320 --> 00:23:15,303
So this is a choice.

531
00:23:15,303 --> 00:23:16,720
With the standard transformer, you

532
00:23:16,720 --> 00:23:19,820
have separate set of weights here and on the next layer.

533
00:23:19,820 --> 00:23:21,760
And I'll tell you how those weights actually

534
00:23:21,760 --> 00:23:24,940
get specified in a minute.

535
00:23:24,940 --> 00:23:27,120
And just to remind you, here was that picture

536
00:23:27,120 --> 00:23:28,500
of the unrolled GNN.

537
00:23:28,500 --> 00:23:31,080
So an unrolled GNN, we have our graph.

538
00:23:31,080 --> 00:23:34,420
And we take these-- now I'm calling them tokens,

539
00:23:34,420 --> 00:23:36,680
they used to be called the node vectors--

540
00:23:36,680 --> 00:23:37,780
and we aggregate.

541
00:23:37,780 --> 00:23:41,040
But this is going to be in a critical difference.

542
00:23:41,040 --> 00:23:44,560
In the GNN, how we aggregate is determined

543
00:23:44,560 --> 00:23:46,513
by the graph connectivity.

544
00:23:46,513 --> 00:23:48,680
It's the adjacency matrix of the graph that tells us

545
00:23:48,680 --> 00:23:50,800
how we do this aggregate, where the arrows are

546
00:23:50,800 --> 00:23:52,675
in that linear combination.

547
00:23:52,675 --> 00:23:55,300
And additionally in the graph, an aggregate might be nonlinear.

548
00:23:55,300 --> 00:23:57,300
It doesn't have to just be a linear combination.

549
00:24:00,146 --> 00:24:04,640
And then we also have a particular way of combining that

550
00:24:04,640 --> 00:24:06,240
through an update step.

551
00:24:06,240 --> 00:24:09,360
But it looks very much like a transformer that I'm showing you

552
00:24:09,360 --> 00:24:11,720
now, the token net version of the transformer,

553
00:24:11,720 --> 00:24:14,750
except that these arrows are not fully connected.

554
00:24:14,750 --> 00:24:18,610
They are determined by the graph structure.

555
00:24:18,610 --> 00:24:22,610
So a transformer is going to be like a graph net,

556
00:24:22,610 --> 00:24:24,410
but it's a fully connected graph net.

557
00:24:24,410 --> 00:24:28,530
So we have all the arrows at every single aggregation layer.

558
00:24:28,530 --> 00:24:31,010
And I'll tell you how those arrows' values get determined,

559
00:24:31,010 --> 00:24:32,670
which is going to be called attention.

560
00:24:32,670 --> 00:24:35,730
But you can think of it as a graph

561
00:24:35,730 --> 00:24:37,490
net over a fully connected graph,

562
00:24:37,490 --> 00:24:39,670
typically without weight-sharing in depth.

563
00:24:42,850 --> 00:24:44,610
Any questions about the relationship

564
00:24:44,610 --> 00:24:47,250
between these different neural networks--

565
00:24:47,250 --> 00:24:53,460
MLPs, CNNs, graph nets, and now this token net I'm describing?

566
00:24:53,460 --> 00:24:55,250
AUDIENCE: Can you talk more about

567
00:24:55,250 --> 00:24:59,415
the token-wise nonlinearity and how it's changing?

568
00:24:59,415 --> 00:25:01,290
PHILLIP ISOLA: So the token-wise nonlinearity

569
00:25:01,290 --> 00:25:03,770
will be typically an MLP, and the parameters

570
00:25:03,770 --> 00:25:06,310
will be learned with backpropagation.

571
00:25:06,310 --> 00:25:10,690
So the free parameters are learned via optimization.

572
00:25:10,690 --> 00:25:12,850
AUDIENCE: And within there in that MLP,

573
00:25:12,850 --> 00:25:17,910
there's point-wise nonlinearities in that as well.

574
00:25:17,910 --> 00:25:18,710
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yes.

575
00:25:18,710 --> 00:25:21,530
So it's like this network of subnetworks.

576
00:25:21,530 --> 00:25:25,930
And in the MLP, there are also point-wise nonlinearities.

577
00:25:25,930 --> 00:25:28,630
And one more?

578
00:25:28,630 --> 00:25:30,290
AUDIENCE: The aggregate functions

579
00:25:30,290 --> 00:25:35,250
are then at least the equivalent of the activation functions

580
00:25:35,250 --> 00:25:36,630
on the transformers then?

581
00:25:39,250 --> 00:25:41,490
PHILLIP ISOLA: The question is, in GNNs

582
00:25:41,490 --> 00:25:44,130
the aggregate function is learned-- or it's parameterized,

583
00:25:44,130 --> 00:25:45,290
and it can be learned.

584
00:25:45,290 --> 00:25:48,050
And in transformers, I didn't tell you

585
00:25:48,050 --> 00:25:51,528
how we're going to learn these weights.

586
00:25:51,528 --> 00:25:53,570
In transformers, that will be done via this thing

587
00:25:53,570 --> 00:25:55,650
called attention, which we'll get to in a minute.

588
00:25:55,650 --> 00:25:59,830
And the token-wise nonlinearity in an MLP is not learned.

589
00:25:59,830 --> 00:26:02,233
The point-wise nonlinearity is not learned, typically.

590
00:26:02,233 --> 00:26:04,150
But in a transformer, it typically is learned.

591
00:26:04,150 --> 00:26:05,742
It's a parameterized MLP.

592
00:26:05,742 --> 00:26:07,950
And in a graph net, I think it would also be learned.

593
00:26:07,950 --> 00:26:09,700
It's this update function, which typically

594
00:26:09,700 --> 00:26:11,800
has learnable parameters.

595
00:26:11,800 --> 00:26:12,300
OK.

596
00:26:16,480 --> 00:26:18,720
And don't worry too much about the semantics here.

597
00:26:18,720 --> 00:26:20,595
Like, it doesn't matter if you call something

598
00:26:20,595 --> 00:26:21,880
a graph net or a transformer.

599
00:26:21,880 --> 00:26:23,540
You should understand the concepts and how there's

600
00:26:23,540 --> 00:26:24,900
these particular building blocks that

601
00:26:24,900 --> 00:26:26,140
can be combined in different ways

602
00:26:26,140 --> 00:26:27,200
to achieve different effects.

603
00:26:27,200 --> 00:26:28,158
And there's trade-offs.

604
00:26:28,158 --> 00:26:30,200
And if you understand that deeply,

605
00:26:30,200 --> 00:26:33,780
then the names are just something

606
00:26:33,780 --> 00:26:35,580
that is secondary to that.

607
00:26:39,460 --> 00:26:42,180
So that was the first new idea was

608
00:26:42,180 --> 00:26:44,300
we're going to operate over tokens, which

609
00:26:44,300 --> 00:26:46,360
are vector-valued units, instead of neurons,

610
00:26:46,360 --> 00:26:48,920
which are scalar-valued units.

611
00:26:48,920 --> 00:26:52,580
The next idea, which is going to actually be technically new,

612
00:26:52,580 --> 00:26:54,020
is going to be attention, which is

613
00:26:54,020 --> 00:26:57,580
how do we take the linear combination exactly

614
00:26:57,580 --> 00:26:58,680
between tokens?

615
00:27:02,020 --> 00:27:03,880
Let's say I have a token net.

616
00:27:03,880 --> 00:27:07,500
What I could do is I could just learn the weights

617
00:27:07,500 --> 00:27:08,640
of the linear combination.

618
00:27:08,640 --> 00:27:10,390
Just like in an MLP, I learned the weights

619
00:27:10,390 --> 00:27:11,860
of the linear combination.

620
00:27:11,860 --> 00:27:15,540
So free parameters I'll draw in blue.

621
00:27:15,540 --> 00:27:17,520
And this could just be a weight matrix,

622
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:19,380
which is going to be three numbers, in this case, because I

623
00:27:19,380 --> 00:27:20,130
have three tokens.

624
00:27:20,130 --> 00:27:23,000
So it would be a three by one weight matrix and one output.

625
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:26,380
Three inputs, one output.

626
00:27:26,380 --> 00:27:29,650
And W will be free parameters that are learnable via backprop.

627
00:27:29,650 --> 00:27:30,400
That's one option.

628
00:27:30,400 --> 00:27:31,067
You can do this.

629
00:27:31,067 --> 00:27:33,620
That will work

630
00:27:33,620 --> 00:27:36,140
But transformers actually are coupled with this other idea

631
00:27:36,140 --> 00:27:37,020
called attention.

632
00:27:37,020 --> 00:27:40,260
And in attention, the weights that you

633
00:27:40,260 --> 00:27:41,840
use to do this linear combination,

634
00:27:41,840 --> 00:27:43,000
now they're not free parameters.

635
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:43,980
So I'm not going to color them in blue.

636
00:27:43,980 --> 00:27:45,160
I'm coloring them in red.

637
00:27:45,160 --> 00:27:46,993
And if you remember in some of the readings,

638
00:27:46,993 --> 00:27:49,200
red was our color for activations.

639
00:27:49,200 --> 00:27:52,878
It turns out the weights I used to combine the tokens

640
00:27:52,878 --> 00:27:54,420
with the linear combination are going

641
00:27:54,420 --> 00:27:59,020
to be coming from activations elsewhere in the network.

642
00:27:59,020 --> 00:28:03,680
So A is going to not be free parameters.

643
00:28:03,680 --> 00:28:05,870
It's going to be a function of your data

644
00:28:05,870 --> 00:28:07,210
that you're processing.

645
00:28:07,210 --> 00:28:09,210
So there's a few different ways you can do that.

646
00:28:09,210 --> 00:28:10,510
We'll go into the full details.

647
00:28:10,510 --> 00:28:13,350
But just think that there's going to be some function F that

648
00:28:13,350 --> 00:28:16,950
takes in your input data you're processing and determines

649
00:28:16,950 --> 00:28:21,210
this linear combination matrix A.

650
00:28:21,210 --> 00:28:24,190
So A is going to be set by some function, which I'll describe,

651
00:28:24,190 --> 00:28:25,050
of the data.

652
00:28:25,050 --> 00:28:26,750
It's not going to be free parameters.

653
00:28:26,750 --> 00:28:30,230
And T out will be just a linear combination of T in,

654
00:28:30,230 --> 00:28:34,550
according to A.

655
00:28:34,550 --> 00:28:40,830
So let's get some intuition for what this attention matrix might

656
00:28:40,830 --> 00:28:44,530
be doing, this attention matrix A. So here's an image.

657
00:28:44,530 --> 00:28:47,510
And let's say I want to process the query, how many animals are

658
00:28:47,510 --> 00:28:48,442
in this photo?

659
00:28:48,442 --> 00:28:51,150
So I told you the intuition is I should look at the three animals

660
00:28:51,150 --> 00:28:52,630
and then count them up.

661
00:28:52,630 --> 00:28:56,110
And that's exactly what we'll do.

662
00:28:56,110 --> 00:29:00,188
So I will attend to-- remember, tokens are representing

663
00:29:00,188 --> 00:29:02,230
patches of the image, at least on the first layer

664
00:29:02,230 --> 00:29:02,930
of processing.

665
00:29:02,930 --> 00:29:04,630
They're representing patches.

666
00:29:04,630 --> 00:29:06,950
So what are the patches I will attend to in this image

667
00:29:06,950 --> 00:29:08,190
to answer this question?

668
00:29:08,190 --> 00:29:10,215
Should be any patch that has an animal.

669
00:29:10,215 --> 00:29:12,590
And it's a little hard to see, but there's a giraffe head

670
00:29:12,590 --> 00:29:14,310
in this one here.

671
00:29:14,310 --> 00:29:20,670
So this is zebra, impala, and two giraffes.

672
00:29:20,670 --> 00:29:23,950
Now, every token is a vector of numbers

673
00:29:23,950 --> 00:29:26,710
that are descriptive of what is going on.

674
00:29:26,710 --> 00:29:31,215
There's some representation of that part of the world.

675
00:29:31,215 --> 00:29:32,590
So at the first layer, it'll just

676
00:29:32,590 --> 00:29:36,150
be the concatenation or the flattened list of all the RGB

677
00:29:36,150 --> 00:29:36,650
values.

678
00:29:36,650 --> 00:29:38,605
But later on, it could be more abstract.

679
00:29:38,605 --> 00:29:40,230
And here I'm just indicating that there

680
00:29:40,230 --> 00:29:41,950
might be one element of the vector which

681
00:29:41,950 --> 00:29:44,670
is an indicator function.

682
00:29:44,670 --> 00:29:48,150
Does this patch contain an animal or not,

683
00:29:48,150 --> 00:29:51,150
or maybe the head of an animal or not?

684
00:29:51,150 --> 00:29:53,270
Because each animal has one head.

685
00:29:53,270 --> 00:29:55,990
And then what I can do is my attention

686
00:29:55,990 --> 00:29:59,150
can be allocating one unit of attention

687
00:29:59,150 --> 00:30:02,620
to each of these tokens, and zero units to any other token.

688
00:30:02,620 --> 00:30:05,520
And then if I take a linear combination with weights of 1

689
00:30:05,520 --> 00:30:08,100
for each of those tokens and 0 for all the other tokens,

690
00:30:08,100 --> 00:30:09,760
that will achieve the correct answer

691
00:30:09,760 --> 00:30:14,000
of outputting a new token vector whose

692
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:17,520
value is 4 in the dimension that counts animals.

693
00:30:17,520 --> 00:30:19,680
So I've just kind of hand constructed here

694
00:30:19,680 --> 00:30:24,200
a transformer, one layer transformer,

695
00:30:24,200 --> 00:30:27,720
that counts the number of animals in the image

696
00:30:27,720 --> 00:30:30,240
by simply attending to animal heads

697
00:30:30,240 --> 00:30:34,200
and then taking a sum over all the things I attended

698
00:30:34,200 --> 00:30:37,180
to where the attribute vector of each thing I'm attending to.

699
00:30:37,180 --> 00:30:40,040
The token vector is just a 1 if there's an animal head, or a 0

700
00:30:40,040 --> 00:30:41,463
otherwise.

701
00:30:41,463 --> 00:30:42,880
Or in fact, in this case, it could

702
00:30:42,880 --> 00:30:44,728
be a 1 no matter what's in the photo,

703
00:30:44,728 --> 00:30:46,520
because the attention already only selected

704
00:30:46,520 --> 00:30:48,222
to look at the animal heads.

705
00:30:48,222 --> 00:30:49,180
Here's another example.

706
00:30:49,180 --> 00:30:50,500
What is the color of the impala?

707
00:30:50,500 --> 00:30:51,833
So what should I attend to here?

708
00:30:51,833 --> 00:30:56,420
I should attend to these tokens, these patches.

709
00:30:56,420 --> 00:30:58,940
And now I have another dimension of my token vector

710
00:30:58,940 --> 00:31:02,120
which maybe represents the average color within that patch.

711
00:31:02,120 --> 00:31:05,040
And if I take the average over those,

712
00:31:05,040 --> 00:31:07,660
I take a weighted sum over the things I'm attending to,

713
00:31:07,660 --> 00:31:10,720
I will output the average color of the impala.

714
00:31:10,720 --> 00:31:13,400
And that will be maybe a good ensembled response

715
00:31:13,400 --> 00:31:15,420
about the answer to this question,

716
00:31:15,420 --> 00:31:16,615
what color is the impala?

717
00:31:16,615 --> 00:31:18,240
So this is just the intuition, but hand

718
00:31:18,240 --> 00:31:20,800
constructing how these operations could actually

719
00:31:20,800 --> 00:31:23,760
answer queries of interest.

720
00:31:23,760 --> 00:31:24,400
Question?

721
00:31:24,400 --> 00:31:27,720
AUDIENCE: How do you interpret the [INAUDIBLE] of the T out?

722
00:31:27,720 --> 00:31:29,592
Is there a [INAUDIBLE]?

723
00:31:29,592 --> 00:31:32,800
PHILLIP ISOLA: T out here is meant to be--

724
00:31:32,800 --> 00:31:35,780
oh, yeah, I suppose that maybe I should use a capital letter,

725
00:31:35,780 --> 00:31:37,107
but just one token output.

726
00:31:37,107 --> 00:31:39,440
It's like I'm just outputting one single token to answer

727
00:31:39,440 --> 00:31:41,420
my question in this example.

728
00:31:43,945 --> 00:31:45,320
So yeah, the more general form is

729
00:31:45,320 --> 00:31:46,987
I'll have an array of tokens represented

730
00:31:46,987 --> 00:31:48,760
by the matrix T, where the tokens are

731
00:31:48,760 --> 00:31:50,760
in the rows of that array.

732
00:31:50,760 --> 00:31:53,238
So here is going to be the mathematical formulation

733
00:31:53,238 --> 00:31:54,780
of the most common kind of attention.

734
00:31:54,780 --> 00:31:57,480
It's called query-key-value attention.

735
00:31:57,480 --> 00:32:00,450
And remember, the transformer is mapping from a set of tokens

736
00:32:00,450 --> 00:32:02,950
to a set of tokens, so T in to T out.

737
00:32:02,950 --> 00:32:05,450
And this will be one layer of the transformer, one attention

738
00:32:05,450 --> 00:32:06,870
layer, so one linear combination.

739
00:32:06,870 --> 00:32:08,410
So how is it going to work?

740
00:32:08,410 --> 00:32:10,710
So I will have a question.

741
00:32:10,710 --> 00:32:12,370
And then I'm going to use the question

742
00:32:12,370 --> 00:32:14,210
to determine the attention.

743
00:32:14,210 --> 00:32:16,930
So the question, the way I determine the attention

744
00:32:16,930 --> 00:32:19,570
is like that function F that produces A.

745
00:32:19,570 --> 00:32:21,190
So here's what it looks like.

746
00:32:21,190 --> 00:32:24,755
So my input signal is a set of tokens.

747
00:32:24,755 --> 00:32:26,130
So it's actually just going to be

748
00:32:26,130 --> 00:32:27,710
thought of as an unordered set.

749
00:32:27,710 --> 00:32:30,770
Just here's a bunch of different patches in the image.

750
00:32:30,770 --> 00:32:35,050
And every token has this vector associated with it.

751
00:32:35,050 --> 00:32:38,610
And the question will also be represented

752
00:32:38,610 --> 00:32:43,250
as a token, which has a vector associated with it.

753
00:32:43,250 --> 00:32:45,970
And I'll have a special operation called query,

754
00:32:45,970 --> 00:32:51,570
which will be to emit some vector based

755
00:32:51,570 --> 00:32:56,710
on the contents of my question's embedding vector.

756
00:32:56,710 --> 00:32:59,990
So the question the question is a token, which is a vector.

757
00:32:59,990 --> 00:33:03,330
And then I can transform that to emit some kind of query.

758
00:33:03,330 --> 00:33:05,210
So that will just be a linear transformation

759
00:33:05,210 --> 00:33:07,290
of that input vector.

760
00:33:07,290 --> 00:33:10,770
And each token in my signal that I'm processing

761
00:33:10,770 --> 00:33:14,970
will have a function called key, which emits something

762
00:33:14,970 --> 00:33:17,590
that the query is meant to try to match against,

763
00:33:17,590 --> 00:33:22,010
to see if the query matches the key.

764
00:33:22,010 --> 00:33:25,490
So tokens represented by a vector,

765
00:33:25,490 --> 00:33:27,690
and they can emit either queries in order

766
00:33:27,690 --> 00:33:29,530
to say what should I attend to, or keys

767
00:33:29,530 --> 00:33:35,930
to say what is my content that my query should match to.

768
00:33:35,930 --> 00:33:38,010
And we will check whether a query matches

769
00:33:38,010 --> 00:33:42,250
a key via a dot product between the key vector

770
00:33:42,250 --> 00:33:44,190
and the query vector.

771
00:33:44,190 --> 00:33:47,750
So this that horizontal line is the key vector.

772
00:33:47,750 --> 00:33:51,710
And that vertical yellow line is the query vector.

773
00:33:51,710 --> 00:33:54,420
And this is just meant to be the dot product, like the matrix

774
00:33:54,420 --> 00:34:01,800
product, of the 1 by D vector times the D by 1 vector.

775
00:34:01,800 --> 00:34:03,200
So what will happen here?

776
00:34:03,200 --> 00:34:07,880
Well, my query is relevant to animal heads.

777
00:34:07,880 --> 00:34:09,780
What is the color of the impala's head?

778
00:34:09,780 --> 00:34:13,699
So my key will match that query if I'm

779
00:34:13,699 --> 00:34:15,199
looking at an animal head.

780
00:34:15,199 --> 00:34:17,620
So the giraffe will be 0.2, but it will match even better

781
00:34:17,620 --> 00:34:18,860
