1178
00:51:44,870 --> 00:51:47,310
can change different [? things. ?]

1179
00:51:47,310 --> 00:51:49,662
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah, OK, I'm not sure I've

1180
00:51:49,662 --> 00:51:51,370
got the first part of the question quite.

1181
00:51:51,370 --> 00:51:54,270
But I think you're asking, shouldn't we

1182
00:51:54,270 --> 00:51:59,450
have a different way of processing language and vision?

1183
00:51:59,450 --> 00:52:01,290
Can you adapt your architecture to that?

1184
00:52:01,290 --> 00:52:04,410
And the answer is, yeah, that is of interest.

1185
00:52:04,410 --> 00:52:07,550
But again, the power of the transformer and the transformer

1186
00:52:07,550 --> 00:52:11,510
paradigm is only put in domain knowledge

1187
00:52:11,510 --> 00:52:13,910
into the first step, plus a few other places,

1188
00:52:13,910 --> 00:52:15,910
like in positional encoding, which we'll get to,

1189
00:52:15,910 --> 00:52:17,310
you can put in domain knowledge.

1190
00:52:17,310 --> 00:52:20,110
And otherwise use a very generic computational framework,

1191
00:52:20,110 --> 00:52:26,402
which has the advantage of just if you homogenize, then

1192
00:52:26,402 --> 00:52:28,110
you can get advantages of everything runs

1193
00:52:28,110 --> 00:52:29,610
on the same commodity hardware.

1194
00:52:29,610 --> 00:52:31,010
The code bases are all the same.

1195
00:52:31,010 --> 00:52:33,905
The lessons are transferable between different modalities.

1196
00:52:33,905 --> 00:52:35,530
So there's a lot of advantages to that.

1197
00:52:35,530 --> 00:52:39,810
But specialization to modality is of interest as well.

1198
00:52:39,810 --> 00:52:41,570
It's just a trade-off, a back and forth.

1199
00:52:41,570 --> 00:52:42,945
How much do you specialize versus

1200
00:52:42,945 --> 00:52:45,870
how much do you make homogeneous approaches?

1201
00:52:45,870 --> 00:52:47,690
I'm not sure I quite answered the question,

1202
00:52:47,690 --> 00:52:50,030
but I think I need to move on to get through everything.

1203
00:52:50,030 --> 00:52:51,910
We can talk after.

1204
00:52:51,910 --> 00:52:56,690
So as I said, the attention layer is, at the end of the day,

1205
00:52:56,690 --> 00:52:58,170
just a linear combination.

1206
00:52:58,170 --> 00:53:01,270
So we can now think of a family of different linear combinations

1207
00:53:01,270 --> 00:53:03,030
of neurons that we've seen.

1208
00:53:03,030 --> 00:53:05,970
So the first one was in the MLP, the fully connected layer,

1209
00:53:05,970 --> 00:53:08,310
where every single edge in the mapping

1210
00:53:08,310 --> 00:53:11,340
from input vector to output vector is a learnable parameter.

1211
00:53:11,340 --> 00:53:13,090
So we have n squared learnable parameters.

1212
00:53:13,090 --> 00:53:16,140
If the input is in Rn and the output is in Rn.

1213
00:53:16,140 --> 00:53:21,040
And we also have some bias terms that we can add, n bias terms.

1214
00:53:21,040 --> 00:53:24,410
In convolution, we have a much lower rank matrix.

1215
00:53:24,410 --> 00:53:26,160
But remember, we said that the convolution

1216
00:53:26,160 --> 00:53:28,680
can be represented as a matrix.

1217
00:53:28,680 --> 00:53:31,640
For a fixed dimensional input, we can represent the convolution

1218
00:53:31,640 --> 00:53:34,600
as a fixed dimensional matrix.

1219
00:53:34,600 --> 00:53:38,120
But the interesting thing is it has this Toeplitz

1220
00:53:38,120 --> 00:53:41,020
structure, where the diagonals all share the same value.

1221
00:53:41,020 --> 00:53:44,000
So the colors indicate the unique values in this matrix.

1222
00:53:44,000 --> 00:53:45,860
So there's far fewer learnable parameters.

1223
00:53:45,860 --> 00:53:47,260
There's only four learnable parameters

1224
00:53:47,260 --> 00:53:48,920
if we include the bias, as opposed to n

1225
00:53:48,920 --> 00:53:51,880
squared learnable parameters, for this toy problem where

1226
00:53:51,880 --> 00:53:54,400
the convolutional kernel size is 3.

1227
00:53:54,400 --> 00:53:58,600
So every three neurons in the input

1228
00:53:58,600 --> 00:54:00,840
map to one neuron in the output.

1229
00:54:00,840 --> 00:54:02,680
And convolution has this nice property

1230
00:54:02,680 --> 00:54:04,800
of translation equivariance, conv

1231
00:54:04,800 --> 00:54:09,760
of trans x is trans of conv x.

1232
00:54:09,760 --> 00:54:11,677
And that comes because of this weight sharing.

1233
00:54:11,677 --> 00:54:12,468
You kind of see it.

1234
00:54:12,468 --> 00:54:13,960
Like, if I translate the input, I'm

1235
00:54:13,960 --> 00:54:16,600
just the same values at a different location

1236
00:54:16,600 --> 00:54:18,440
in that matrix.

1237
00:54:18,440 --> 00:54:21,520
So what is the transformer attention matrix

1238
00:54:21,520 --> 00:54:23,920
look like with this notation?

1239
00:54:23,920 --> 00:54:25,838
So what do you think?

1240
00:54:25,838 --> 00:54:28,380
This is probably pretty tricky to work out just in your head.

1241
00:54:28,380 --> 00:54:30,297
But what do you think it's going to look like?

1242
00:54:30,297 --> 00:54:32,100
I'm going to draw it on the next animation.

1243
00:54:32,100 --> 00:54:33,100
So anyone want to guess?

1244
00:54:33,100 --> 00:54:36,440
Like, what is the transformer's matrix of unique values

1245
00:54:36,440 --> 00:54:37,840
going to look like?

1246
00:54:37,840 --> 00:54:38,900
OK, yeah, let's go here.

1247
00:54:38,900 --> 00:54:41,192
AUDIENCE: So it's not going to necessarily look sparse,

1248
00:54:41,192 --> 00:54:42,640
but it should be low rank.

1249
00:54:42,640 --> 00:54:44,955
PHILLIP ISOLA: So it will be low rank.

1250
00:54:44,955 --> 00:54:46,580
You said it's not going to look sparse.

1251
00:54:46,580 --> 00:54:48,830
I think it does look sparse, but we'll see, we'll see.

1252
00:54:48,830 --> 00:54:50,640
Yeah, OK, any others?

1253
00:54:50,640 --> 00:54:54,640
It's going to be some low-rank sparse matrix.

1254
00:54:54,640 --> 00:54:56,140
It's a little hard to work it out.

1255
00:54:56,140 --> 00:54:59,460
So I also have to tell you how I'm notating things.

1256
00:54:59,460 --> 00:55:02,560
So I mean, it's not like you were wrong.

1257
00:55:02,560 --> 00:55:04,320
So I'm going to notate things where

1258
00:55:04,320 --> 00:55:06,760
I'm going to take the first three neurons

1259
00:55:06,760 --> 00:55:08,583
and call that one token, and the next three

1260
00:55:08,583 --> 00:55:09,500
neurons another token.

1261
00:55:09,500 --> 00:55:12,550
So I'm factorizing the input signal into these two tokens

1262
00:55:12,550 --> 00:55:13,050
now.

1263
00:55:13,050 --> 00:55:17,050
And then those two tokens get to attend and interact

1264
00:55:17,050 --> 00:55:18,610
with each other.

1265
00:55:18,610 --> 00:55:22,410
But that just corresponds to using the same set of weights

1266
00:55:22,410 --> 00:55:24,630
for each element of the token vector,

1267
00:55:24,630 --> 00:55:27,890
because a linear combination of rows of a matrix

1268
00:55:27,890 --> 00:55:30,170
is using the same weights for every element of the row

1269
00:55:30,170 --> 00:55:31,950
when I'm taking that combination.

1270
00:55:31,950 --> 00:55:33,310
So you can work it out.

1271
00:55:33,310 --> 00:55:35,685
Think of it for yourself, but this is what it looks like.

1272
00:55:35,685 --> 00:55:38,730
So transformers have these layers or linear layers.

1273
00:55:38,730 --> 00:55:42,630
Of course, the weights are coming from queries and values.

1274
00:55:42,630 --> 00:55:44,338
So there's another mechanism on the side.

1275
00:55:44,338 --> 00:55:46,922
But at the end of the day, it's just like this low-rank sparse

1276
00:55:46,922 --> 00:55:47,550
transformation.

1277
00:55:47,550 --> 00:55:49,810
It has fewer linear learnable parameters, which

1278
00:55:49,810 --> 00:55:53,443
is advantageous in some ways.

1279
00:55:53,443 --> 00:55:55,110
And then the other interesting property,

1280
00:55:55,110 --> 00:55:56,568
which I'll describe in a minute, is

1281
00:55:56,568 --> 00:56:00,770
that transformers are equivariant with permutations

1282
00:56:00,770 --> 00:56:01,510
of the input.

