1293
00:56:27,850 --> 00:56:29,090
It's in the reading.

1294
00:56:29,090 --> 00:56:30,110
There's a section on it.

1295
00:56:30,110 --> 00:56:33,370
But the idea is that, rather than just having one attention

1296
00:56:33,370 --> 00:56:35,910
layer, I can have k attention layers in parallel,

1297
00:56:35,910 --> 00:56:37,167
and then I can aggregate them.

1298
00:56:37,167 --> 00:56:39,750
And each attention layer can be attending to different things.

1299
00:56:39,750 --> 00:56:42,050
One can learn to attend to shapes

1300
00:56:42,050 --> 00:56:46,250
and one can learn to attend to textures, for example.

1301
00:56:46,250 --> 00:56:50,510
OK, so here is the complete vision transformer architecture.

1302
00:56:50,510 --> 00:56:55,510
This is the standard way of processing spatial signals.

1303
00:56:55,510 --> 00:56:58,490
So images in particular, but also audio and other things

1304
00:56:58,490 --> 00:57:00,810
can be used with the same architecture.

1305
00:57:00,810 --> 00:57:03,270
And there's only a few things that you haven't seen.

1306
00:57:03,270 --> 00:57:06,490
So it's going to be a set of tokens,

1307
00:57:06,490 --> 00:57:08,760
multi-headed self-attention, which is MSA.

1308
00:57:08,760 --> 00:57:12,140
So that's just multiple runs of attention, then combining them

1309
00:57:12,140 --> 00:57:15,780
all together, and then point-wise nonlinearity,

1310
00:57:15,780 --> 00:57:17,380
which is going to be an MLP.

1311
00:57:17,380 --> 00:57:19,120
The blue are the learnable parameters.

1312
00:57:19,120 --> 00:57:20,560
Everything else is not learnable.

1313
00:57:20,560 --> 00:57:23,460
So the queries and the keys and the values,

1314
00:57:23,460 --> 00:57:27,125
which are indicated by this F, well, that's really the queries,

1315
00:57:27,125 --> 00:57:28,500
but there's also keys and values.

1316
00:57:28,500 --> 00:57:29,520
That's learnable.

1317
00:57:29,520 --> 00:57:32,320
And the parameters of the token-wise MLP are learnable.

1318
00:57:32,320 --> 00:57:33,780
Everything else is not.

1319
00:57:33,780 --> 00:57:36,420
These pluses here are residual connections.

1320
00:57:36,420 --> 00:57:39,460
So I think in the CNN lecture, we talked about ResNets.

1321
00:57:39,460 --> 00:57:43,360
So we'll just take an identity pass around all this processing.

1322
00:57:43,360 --> 00:57:45,780
And that has some advantages.

1323
00:57:45,780 --> 00:57:47,780
And then there's one other little layer,

1324
00:57:47,780 --> 00:57:49,580
which I'm labeling token norm.

1325
00:57:49,580 --> 00:57:53,820
It's often more commonly called in the literature LayerNorm.

1326
00:57:53,820 --> 00:57:58,380
There's not really an obvious kind of layer structure here.

1327
00:57:58,380 --> 00:58:02,220
Really, this operation is just taking the vector

1328
00:58:02,220 --> 00:58:06,452
and normalizing it to have zero mean and unit variance.

1329
00:58:06,452 --> 00:58:08,160
So I'm taking the elements of this vector

1330
00:58:08,160 --> 00:58:09,702
and I'm just dividing by the variance

1331
00:58:09,702 --> 00:58:11,700
and subtracting out the mean.

1332
00:58:11,700 --> 00:58:16,872
So that connects a little bit to what Jeremy was talking about

1333
00:58:16,872 --> 00:58:18,580
and what you've done on your problem sets

1334
00:58:18,580 --> 00:58:20,413
or what you're working on your problem sets,

1335
00:58:20,413 --> 00:58:24,860
where it's good to normalize activations and weight

1336
00:58:24,860 --> 00:58:27,960
updates in your network for a lot of reasons.

1337
00:58:27,960 --> 00:58:29,980
One reason that Jeremy alluded to

1338
00:58:29,980 --> 00:58:33,140
is, for stability of optimization

1339
00:58:33,140 --> 00:58:36,060
and taking the steepest descent direction,

1340
00:58:36,060 --> 00:58:37,628
you need to think about the norms.

1341
00:58:37,628 --> 00:58:39,420
And you might want to normalize your weight

1342
00:58:39,420 --> 00:58:41,120
updates in a particular norm.

1343
00:58:41,120 --> 00:58:42,643
And your problem set goes into that.

1344
00:58:42,643 --> 00:58:44,560
Here we're not normalizing the weight updates.

1345
00:58:44,560 --> 00:58:46,040
We're normalizing the activations.

1346
00:58:46,040 --> 00:58:49,300
But you can understand that normalizing activations will

1347
00:58:49,300 --> 00:58:52,500
have a consequence on changing the size of the weight updates.

1348
00:58:52,500 --> 00:58:56,260
Because if I backprop through normalizing the activations,

1349
00:58:56,260 --> 00:58:58,380
you can see that the weight update

1350
00:58:58,380 --> 00:59:03,580
will be a function of the scale of the activations.

1351
00:59:03,580 --> 00:59:06,490
So LayerNorm has desirable optimization properties.

1352
00:59:06,490 --> 00:59:09,430
Exactly what those are is basically open science.

1353
00:59:09,430 --> 00:59:12,770
And it's not quite clear why LayerNorm is right thing to do,

1354
00:59:12,770 --> 00:59:14,290
but it's what people do.

1355
00:59:17,230 --> 00:59:19,450
So you'll implement transformers on your problem set,

1356
00:59:19,450 --> 00:59:23,750
but I want to quickly walk through a kind of pseudocode

1357
00:59:23,750 --> 00:59:26,270
of it just to show you how simple these things are.

1358
00:59:26,270 --> 00:59:27,530
They're so easy to implement.

1359
00:59:27,530 --> 00:59:29,830
That's one of the reasons why people like them.

1360
00:59:29,830 --> 00:59:34,190
So here is a transformer in the vision transformer style

1361
00:59:34,190 --> 00:59:40,590
that is processing a set of tokens T.

1362
00:59:40,590 --> 00:59:41,930
So we first tokenize the input.

1363
00:59:41,930 --> 00:59:43,597
That might be domain-specific knowledge,

1364
00:59:43,597 --> 00:59:46,052
like break up your image into patches.

1365
00:59:46,052 --> 00:59:47,510
And then for each layer, we're just

1366
00:59:47,510 --> 00:59:50,620
going to run the same operation.

1367
00:59:50,620 --> 00:59:52,370
We might have different weights per layer,

1368
00:59:52,370 --> 00:59:54,070
but otherwise the same operation.

1369
00:59:54,070 --> 01:00:01,750
We will take the matrix multiply of the LayerNorm of the tokens,

1370
01:00:01,750 --> 01:00:06,710
times like this outer product of the key query

1371
01:00:06,710 --> 01:00:09,390
and value matrices with the token matrix.

1372
01:00:09,390 --> 01:00:10,870
And we're LayerNorming it.

1373
01:00:10,870 --> 01:00:14,250
Then we get key query and value matrices.

1374
01:00:14,250 --> 01:00:19,390
And then we'll just take the matrix multiply of Q times K

1375
01:00:19,390 --> 01:00:22,310
transpose, divided by the dimensionality, which here I'm

1376
01:00:22,310 --> 01:00:25,950
noting as D, the dimensionality of the key query and value

1377
01:00:25,950 --> 01:00:26,830
vectors.

1378
01:00:26,830 --> 01:00:29,050
And then I'll add residual connections,

1379
01:00:29,050 --> 01:00:30,970
and take the linear combination, other matmul.

1380
01:00:30,970 --> 01:00:32,510
So everything is just matmuls and

1381
01:00:32,510 --> 01:00:34,090
a few other simple operators.

1382
01:00:34,090 --> 01:00:37,190
And this maps wonderfully onto modern compute

1383
01:00:37,190 --> 01:00:39,710
that loves matrix multiplies.

1384
01:00:39,710 --> 01:00:41,570
OK, so really simple.

1385
01:00:41,570 --> 01:00:43,250
And that should work.

1386
01:00:45,692 --> 01:00:47,150
So there's one more new idea I have

1387
01:00:47,150 --> 01:00:50,400
to get to before the end of the lecture.

1388
01:00:50,400 --> 01:00:52,150
Well, there's a few more things to get to,

1389
01:00:52,150 --> 01:00:55,090
but one more big new idea, which is positional encodings.

1390
01:00:55,090 --> 01:00:56,040
It's not a new idea.

1391
01:00:56,040 --> 01:00:58,290
Again, it's been seen in a lot of older architectures,

1392
01:00:58,290 --> 01:01:01,000
but it was one of the key things that

1393
01:01:01,000 --> 01:01:03,680
made transformers stand out.

1394
01:01:03,680 --> 01:01:10,240
So the first thing to know is that transformers

1395
01:01:10,240 --> 01:01:16,160
are permutation equivariant, just like graph nets.

1396
01:01:16,160 --> 01:01:19,520
So if I permute the input sequence of tokens,

1397
01:01:19,520 --> 01:01:21,320
then you can work out for yourself

1398
01:01:21,320 --> 01:01:24,040
that if I permute it, well, it's like taking T2

1399
01:01:24,040 --> 01:01:26,480
into the place of T1, then the output

1400
01:01:26,480 --> 01:01:29,020
will also change the order.

1401
01:01:29,020 --> 01:01:33,800
So the output representation of T2 will now be in this vector,

1402
01:01:33,800 --> 01:01:35,620
as opposed to in the second vector.

1403
01:01:35,620 --> 01:01:38,460
So permuting the input permutes the output

1404
01:01:38,460 --> 01:01:41,400
So transformers are essentially a set-to-set mapping.

1405
01:01:41,400 --> 01:01:45,340
An unordered set maps to an unordered set.

1406
01:01:45,340 --> 01:01:49,140
The ordering of the tokens doesn't matter.

1407
01:01:49,140 --> 01:01:52,360
So you can see this, because point-wise, token-wise

1408
01:01:52,360 --> 01:01:55,080
nonlinearity F, it just applies to every token independently

1409
01:01:55,080 --> 01:01:55,820
and identically.

1410
01:01:55,820 --> 01:01:57,440
So of course, if I change the order of the tokens,

1411
01:01:57,440 --> 01:01:58,940
it doesn't change anything.

1412
01:01:58,940 --> 01:02:00,865
I'm just treating them all independently.

1413
01:02:00,865 --> 01:02:02,240
And you can work out for yourself

1414
01:02:02,240 --> 01:02:04,920
that attention is permutation invariant.

1415
01:02:04,920 --> 01:02:07,340
Because again, attention is, like, every pair of tokens,

1416
01:02:07,340 --> 01:02:08,757
how much they attend to each other

1417
01:02:08,757 --> 01:02:11,820
will just be determined based on the values in the token vectors,

1418
01:02:11,820 --> 01:02:13,720
not the order.

1419
01:02:13,720 --> 01:02:16,720
So because transformers are just point-wise,

1420
01:02:16,720 --> 01:02:18,540
token-wise nonlinearity and attention,

1421
01:02:18,540 --> 01:02:20,640
the whole thing is permutation equivariant.

1422
01:02:20,640 --> 01:02:22,640
So transformer permute the input is

1423
01:02:22,640 --> 01:02:24,900
equal to permute the transformer of the input.

1424
01:02:27,840 --> 01:02:31,960
So now, positional codes-- well, remember,

1425
01:02:31,960 --> 01:02:34,320
if we want convolutional networks to not

1426
01:02:34,320 --> 01:02:39,480
be translation equivariant, we tell the filter

1427
01:02:39,480 --> 01:02:40,780
where I am in the image.

1428
01:02:40,780 --> 01:02:42,620
And then it can make a different decision based on where I am.

1429
01:02:42,620 --> 01:02:44,420
It's no longer translation equivariant.

1430
01:02:44,420 --> 01:02:45,800
We saw that a few times.

1431
01:02:45,800 --> 01:02:48,480
So now you can do exactly the same thing with transformers.

1432
01:02:48,480 --> 01:02:50,560
If I don't want it to be permutation equivariant,

1433
01:02:50,560 --> 01:02:52,060
which oftentimes you actually don't.

1434
01:02:52,060 --> 01:02:53,720
Oftentimes, the order matters.

1435
01:02:53,720 --> 01:02:56,320
If I'm processing a sentence, the earlier sentences

1436
01:02:56,320 --> 01:02:57,355
have causal impact.

1437
01:02:57,355 --> 01:02:58,730
The earlier words in the sentence

1438
01:02:58,730 --> 01:03:02,270
are causally determining the next words,

1439
01:03:02,270 --> 01:03:05,010
but there's not quite the anti-causal direction.

1440
01:03:05,010 --> 01:03:06,230
The statistics are different.

1441
01:03:06,230 --> 01:03:07,970
It's not like a reversible sequence

1442
01:03:07,970 --> 01:03:10,690
where permutation invariant statistical process.

1443
01:03:10,690 --> 01:03:13,770
So anyway, I can concatenate each token

1444
01:03:13,770 --> 01:03:16,370
with a value that tells me where it

1445
01:03:16,370 --> 01:03:18,730
came from in the input signal.

1446
01:03:18,730 --> 01:03:21,430
What position was it in the input sentence?

1447
01:03:21,430 --> 01:03:24,490
What location was it in the input image?

1448
01:03:24,490 --> 01:03:27,750
And here's how you do that typically.

1449
01:03:27,750 --> 01:03:30,970
Not typically-- but this is the vanilla way of doing that.

1450
01:03:30,970 --> 01:03:33,630
There's a lot of more advanced ways of doing it.

1451
01:03:33,630 --> 01:03:36,370
But if I want to tell this patch here

1452
01:03:36,370 --> 01:03:39,570
on the giraffe's head what position it's at,

1453
01:03:39,570 --> 01:03:43,050
I could encode the xy location in Cartesian coordinates

1454
01:03:43,050 --> 01:03:46,170
of that patch, but I'll typically

1455
01:03:46,170 --> 01:03:50,530
represent this on a Fourier basis where I will say,

1456
01:03:50,530 --> 01:03:55,150
what is the value of a sine function at that location?

1457
01:03:55,150 --> 01:03:57,930
And I'll do that for different sines and cosines,

1458
01:03:57,930 --> 01:04:01,650
or in the vertical and the horizontal direction.

1459
01:04:01,650 --> 01:04:04,790
And this is just like an encoding of the position,

1460
01:04:04,790 --> 01:04:08,450
but in terms of a different basis

1461
01:04:08,450 --> 01:04:11,490
as opposed to being just like the Cartesian grid.

1462
01:04:11,490 --> 01:04:15,650
So these values tell me where I am in the image.

1463
01:04:15,650 --> 01:04:18,810
The book chapter goes into some intuition

1464
01:04:18,810 --> 01:04:21,330
about why Fourier positional encoding is

1465
01:04:21,330 --> 01:04:23,110
advantageous over other types.

1466
01:04:23,110 --> 01:04:26,770
But this is also an open science question.

1467
01:04:26,770 --> 01:04:27,990
This one works pretty well.

1468
01:04:30,730 --> 01:04:34,130
And for whatever signal you have,

1469
01:04:34,130 --> 01:04:35,650
there's a lot of domain expertise

1470
01:04:35,650 --> 01:04:37,590
that can come into this part of the problem.

1471
01:04:37,590 --> 01:04:39,257
How you define the positional encodings.

1472
01:04:39,257 --> 01:04:43,410
Because that's the part where you tell the system what

1473
01:04:43,410 --> 01:04:46,637
it means to be local for your domain.

1474
01:04:46,637 --> 01:04:48,970
You give it the inductive bias of what locality actually

1475
01:04:48,970 --> 01:04:49,870
represents.

1476
01:04:49,870 --> 01:04:53,122
So for processing data on a globe,

1477
01:04:53,122 --> 01:04:54,580
I can do positional encoding that's

1478
01:04:54,580 --> 01:04:56,380
like latitude and longitude, but maybe

1479
01:04:56,380 --> 01:04:59,760
on some kind of Fourier basis over spherical harmonics,

1480
01:04:59,760 --> 01:05:02,820
over sinusoids on the sphere.

1481
01:05:02,820 --> 01:05:05,420
For graphs, one of the typical positional encodings

1482
01:05:05,420 --> 01:05:10,640
is onto the eigenbasis of the graph Laplacian.

1483
01:05:10,640 --> 01:05:13,160
So we mentioned this a little bit in the GNN lecture.

1484
01:05:13,160 --> 01:05:15,220
But basically, I can take a graph

1485
01:05:15,220 --> 01:05:18,400
and I can compute something called the graph Laplacian.

1486
01:05:18,400 --> 01:05:20,580
And I can look at the eigenvectors

1487
01:05:20,580 --> 01:05:25,260
of that, this thing that tells me location in the graph,

1488
01:05:25,260 --> 01:05:27,220
canonical location in the graph.

1489
01:05:27,220 --> 01:05:35,420
And the coordinates, some of these eigenvectors of this graph

1490
01:05:35,420 --> 01:05:39,080
Laplacian are described by these colors on the nodes.

1491
01:05:39,080 --> 01:05:43,368
And so the first eigenvector is this one.

1492
01:05:43,368 --> 01:05:44,160
It's really smooth.

1493
01:05:44,160 --> 01:05:46,520
It's like, where am I globally?

1494
01:05:46,520 --> 01:05:49,380
And then other eigenvectors are, like, high frequency, almost

1495
01:05:49,380 --> 01:05:51,992
like a Fourier basis.

1496
01:05:51,992 --> 01:05:53,200
This is all domain knowledge.

1497
01:05:53,200 --> 01:05:56,780
It's not stuff that you need to know intimately, unless you're

1498
01:05:56,780 --> 01:05:58,392
working on that domain.

1499
01:05:58,392 --> 01:06:00,100
But this is just to say you can introduce

1500
01:06:00,100 --> 01:06:04,500
a lot of domain knowledge into positional encodings.

1501
01:06:04,500 --> 01:06:08,245
So those are the three pieces for the transformer.

1502
01:06:08,245 --> 01:06:09,620
But there's one last thing, which

1503
01:06:09,620 --> 01:06:13,180
is a lot of you will have seen large language models

1504
01:06:13,180 --> 01:06:15,700
and already heard about transformers

1505
01:06:15,700 --> 01:06:17,200
in the context of language modeling.

1506
01:06:17,200 --> 01:06:19,100
So I want to tell you how transformers

1507
01:06:19,100 --> 01:06:21,060
map onto language models, because there's one

1508
01:06:21,060 --> 01:06:23,700
extra piece that is important.

1509
01:06:23,700 --> 01:06:26,420
And it's called causal attention.

1510
01:06:26,420 --> 01:06:29,420
So first, we're going to come back

1511
01:06:29,420 --> 01:06:31,660
to autoregressive models and generative

1512
01:06:31,660 --> 01:06:34,392
models of language and other signals a little later.

1513
01:06:34,392 --> 01:06:36,600
And one of the kinds will be an autoregressive model.

1514
01:06:36,600 --> 01:06:38,308
But I'll tell you briefly what it is now,

1515
01:06:38,308 --> 01:06:39,558
because it's extremely simple.

1516
01:06:39,558 --> 01:06:41,558
And a lot of you already know this, because it's

1517
01:06:41,558 --> 01:06:42,360
all over the media.

1518
01:06:42,360 --> 01:06:45,620
This is how ChatGPT works, and so forth.

1519
01:06:45,620 --> 01:06:48,300
You take a sequence of words and you simply

1520
01:06:48,300 --> 01:06:52,690
try to predict what is the next word in that sequence.

1521
01:06:52,690 --> 01:06:56,350
So "once upon a time," and then you do the same thing

1522
01:06:56,350 --> 01:06:59,830
autoregressively, which means you just take your prediction

1523
01:06:59,830 --> 01:07:02,923
and concatenate it or post-pend the input sequence with

1524
01:07:02,923 --> 01:07:04,590
your prediction, and then keep on going.

1525
01:07:04,590 --> 01:07:08,270
Now predict the next word and the next word.

1526
01:07:08,270 --> 01:07:10,590
And you can train such a model, because you

1527
01:07:10,590 --> 01:07:12,750
will train the predictions to match

1528
01:07:12,750 --> 01:07:16,270
the data set of actual text that you observe from the training

1529
01:07:16,270 --> 01:07:18,270
set.

1530
01:07:18,270 --> 01:07:20,070
So we'll say, "once upon a time,"

1531
01:07:20,070 --> 01:07:23,135
and you can watch Claude or Llama,

1532
01:07:23,135 --> 01:07:25,510
or whatever your favorite LLM, you can watch it write out

1533
01:07:25,510 --> 01:07:26,010
things.

1534
01:07:26,010 --> 01:07:28,510
It's doing exactly this.

1535
01:07:28,510 --> 01:07:31,430
We'll talk more about those later.

1536
01:07:31,430 --> 01:07:34,030
And you don't have to do autoregression

1537
01:07:34,030 --> 01:07:37,090
in time's arrow order.

1538
01:07:37,090 --> 01:07:38,910
You can do it in any order you want.

1539
01:07:38,910 --> 01:07:40,190
But that's just a detail.

1540
01:07:40,190 --> 01:07:42,690
So as I said, you have a training set,

1541
01:07:42,690 --> 01:07:45,930
which is sequences and completions to the sequence.

1542
01:07:45,930 --> 01:07:49,110
This is just text online for language models.

1543
01:07:49,110 --> 01:07:51,790
And you are then going to use supervised

1544
01:07:51,790 --> 01:07:54,950
learning to try to classify what is

1545
01:07:54,950 --> 01:07:57,710
the next word in a vocabulary of possible words.

1546
01:07:57,710 --> 01:08:00,430
And then at test time when you're going to sample new

1547
01:08:00,430 --> 01:08:03,570
sentences, you'll just say, "colorless green ideas sleep,"

1548
01:08:03,570 --> 01:08:05,647
it will predict, based on my training data,

1549
01:08:05,647 --> 01:08:06,730
what might have come next.

1550
01:08:06,730 --> 01:08:10,470
And maybe "furiously" came next.

1551
01:08:10,470 --> 01:08:13,170
It's a quote from Noem Chomsky.

1552
01:08:17,350 --> 01:08:18,649
So that's how GPT works.

1553
01:08:18,649 --> 01:08:21,630
GPT is Generative Pre-trained Transformer.

1554
01:08:21,630 --> 01:08:24,990
So it's a transformer.

1555
01:08:24,990 --> 01:08:27,630
And it's an autoregressive transformer.

1556
01:08:27,630 --> 01:08:30,470
So here's my normal transformer that I'm drawing,

1557
01:08:30,470 --> 01:08:31,649
where it's self-attention.

1558
01:08:31,649 --> 01:08:35,229
So the input sequence of tokens that represent the words,

1559
01:08:35,229 --> 01:08:37,390
or maybe subparts of the words, get

1560
01:08:37,390 --> 01:08:39,590
to choose which of them to attend to,

1561
01:08:39,590 --> 01:08:41,970
update their representations, finally

1562
01:08:41,970 --> 01:08:44,630
at the very end come up with a prediction for the next item

1563
01:08:44,630 --> 01:08:46,240
in the sequence.

1564
01:08:46,240 --> 01:08:49,040
And every time I run this, I will

1565
01:08:49,040 --> 01:08:51,500
get a prediction of the next item in the sequence,

1566
01:08:51,500 --> 01:08:56,760
and I'll shift the input over by one and repeat the process.

1567
01:08:56,760 --> 01:09:03,760
So there's a little issue here, which is that if I want to train

1568
01:09:03,760 --> 01:09:07,120
such a system, I want it to be the case that my prediction

1569
01:09:07,120 --> 01:09:10,000
for the word "furiously" only depends on the previous items

1570
01:09:10,000 --> 01:09:11,160
in the sequence.

1571
01:09:11,160 --> 01:09:13,279
Now with this architecture I'm showing here,

1572
01:09:13,279 --> 01:09:14,979
the input has the word "furiously."

1573
01:09:14,979 --> 01:09:17,399
And it could just learn an identity connection to say, oh,

1574
01:09:17,399 --> 01:09:18,580
I'm predicting "furiously."

1575
01:09:18,580 --> 01:09:20,830
And then my input already told me what the answer was.

1576
01:09:20,830 --> 01:09:21,600
That'd be wrong.

1577
01:09:21,600 --> 01:09:23,380
If I'm predicting the word "ideas,"

1578
01:09:23,380 --> 01:09:25,553
I need to only be able to look at the previous items

1579
01:09:25,553 --> 01:09:26,220
in the sequence.

1580
01:09:26,220 --> 01:09:27,595
I can't look into the future if I

1581
01:09:27,595 --> 01:09:31,080
want to be able to write a sentence in time's order.

1582
01:09:31,080 --> 01:09:35,840
So what we do is we do what's called masking, causal masking

1583
01:09:35,840 --> 01:09:37,560
of our attention matrix.

1584
01:09:37,560 --> 01:09:43,640
And that just means that every output token can only

1585
01:09:43,640 --> 01:09:45,979
attend to earlier tokens in the sequence.

1586
01:09:45,979 --> 01:09:47,340
So the order does matter.

1587
01:09:47,340 --> 01:09:49,300
This is no longer permutation equivariant.

1588
01:09:49,300 --> 01:09:51,040
Now the order actually is mattering

1589
01:09:51,040 --> 01:09:53,240
via the masking operation.

1590
01:09:53,240 --> 01:09:57,848
And so now the attention matrix looks like a masked matrix.

1591
01:09:57,848 --> 01:10:00,140
And that just means some of the arrows are not allowed.

1592
01:10:00,140 --> 01:10:01,800
They're just set to zero.

1593
01:10:01,800 --> 01:10:06,200
So the arrows at the top here exactly match the masking

1594
01:10:06,200 --> 01:10:07,220
at the bottom here.

1595
01:10:07,220 --> 01:10:09,240
And I only allowed to learn, or I'm only

1596
01:10:09,240 --> 01:10:11,085
allowed to use these values.

1597
01:10:11,085 --> 01:10:12,960
Meaning, I'm only allowed to attend to things

1598
01:10:12,960 --> 01:10:14,497
previous in the sequence.

1599
01:10:14,497 --> 01:10:16,080
And this allows me to make predictions

1600
01:10:16,080 --> 01:10:18,038
that are causal that only depend on information

1601
01:10:18,038 --> 01:10:19,220
previous in the sequence.

1602
01:10:22,080 --> 01:10:25,520
So this is what a multi-layer transformer

1603
01:10:25,520 --> 01:10:28,120
looks like with causal masking.

1604
01:10:28,120 --> 01:10:31,992
On the first layer, I am trying to predict the fourth item

1605
01:10:31,992 --> 01:10:33,700
in the sequence given the previous three.

1606
01:10:33,700 --> 01:10:38,670
So I will not observe the fourth item, because otherwise I

1607
01:10:38,670 --> 01:10:39,420
would be cheating.

1608
01:10:39,420 --> 01:10:40,800
And it would be too easy.

1609
01:10:40,800 --> 01:10:42,650
But then after the first layer, then I

1610
01:10:42,650 --> 01:10:45,270
can just use a mask that looks like this.

1611
01:10:45,270 --> 01:10:48,708
Every token attends to itself and all the previous tokens.

1612
01:10:48,708 --> 01:10:51,250
And this will achieve that the receptive field of this output

1613
01:10:51,250 --> 01:10:52,970
token that will predict the next word

1614
01:10:52,970 --> 01:10:54,535
can only see every previous word.

1615
01:10:54,535 --> 01:10:56,410
And the receptive field for this output token

1616
01:10:56,410 --> 01:10:58,570
that I could use to predict the second-to-last word

1617
01:10:58,570 --> 01:11:01,015
will only see everything previous to itself.

1618
01:11:01,015 --> 01:11:02,890
And therefore I can just supervise this thing

1619
01:11:02,890 --> 01:11:06,290
to output a sequence of words.

1620
01:11:06,290 --> 01:11:08,050
And every word in that output sequence

1621
01:11:08,050 --> 01:11:09,830
will only depend on the previous words.

1622
01:11:09,830 --> 01:11:14,310
And this is an efficient way of training such a system.

1623
01:11:14,310 --> 01:11:16,550
I'm going a little quickly for the sake of time,

1624
01:11:16,550 --> 01:11:19,170
but there's more details on this in the book chapter.

1625
01:11:19,170 --> 01:11:21,170
This lecture follows the chapter almost exactly,

1626
01:11:21,170 --> 01:11:24,990
because finally I had all the material ready for this year.

1627
01:11:27,795 --> 01:11:30,170
I'm sorry to say, this is just probably the worst diagram

1628
01:11:30,170 --> 01:11:32,690
in all of science, but it's also the best paper

1629
01:11:32,690 --> 01:11:33,550
in all of science.

1630
01:11:33,550 --> 01:11:35,670
So it's like they balance each other out.

1631
01:11:35,670 --> 01:11:38,090
"Attention Is All You Need," it's a super important paper,

1632
01:11:38,090 --> 01:11:41,250
because it's the one that defined the transformer

1633
01:11:41,250 --> 01:11:42,190
architecture.

1634
01:11:42,190 --> 01:11:45,850
Again, it's not new ideas-- it's just a particular recipe that

1635
01:11:45,850 --> 01:11:49,730
is novel in its details, but works incredibly well,

1636
01:11:49,730 --> 01:11:52,162
and a few new ideas here and there.

1637
01:11:52,162 --> 01:11:53,870
And so this paper, just super important--

1638
01:11:53,870 --> 01:11:54,790
"Attention Is All You Need."

1639
01:11:54,790 --> 01:11:56,330
And a lot of you will come across

1640
01:11:56,330 --> 01:11:57,710
or will have seen this diagram.

1641
01:11:57,710 --> 01:12:01,970
This is their diagram for what a transformer is.

1642
01:12:01,970 --> 01:12:03,030
Super confusing to me.

1643
01:12:03,030 --> 01:12:05,990
I don't know why they call this like a feedforward layer.

1644
01:12:05,990 --> 01:12:06,950
But what is it?

1645
01:12:06,950 --> 01:12:09,890
So this is the token-wise nonlinearity.

1646
01:12:09,890 --> 01:12:11,670
That box in blue they call feedforward.

1647
01:12:11,670 --> 01:12:15,890
So it's like just applying independently and identically

1648
01:12:15,890 --> 01:12:19,130
an MLP to every single token vector.

1649
01:12:19,130 --> 01:12:20,310
What is this thing here?

1650
01:12:20,310 --> 01:12:22,350
This is that autoregressive part that I was describing.

1651
01:12:22,350 --> 01:12:24,110
So that has nothing to do with really transformers.

1652
01:12:24,110 --> 01:12:26,050
That's more about autoregressive modeling.

1653
01:12:26,050 --> 01:12:29,790
So they factor those two ideas separately,

1654
01:12:29,790 --> 01:12:31,770
but they put them together in the first paper.

1655
01:12:31,770 --> 01:12:37,860
So that's just saying, shifted right.

1656
01:12:37,860 --> 01:12:40,380
Shifted right is this thing where I just

1657
01:12:40,380 --> 01:12:42,960
shifted the input sequence to the left, I guess,

1658
01:12:42,960 --> 01:12:44,300
in my diagram.

1659
01:12:44,300 --> 01:12:47,220
And positional encoding, that's meant

1660
01:12:47,220 --> 01:12:49,900
to be like a sinusoid positional encoding.

1661
01:12:49,900 --> 01:12:52,080
Then all of these things, add and norm,

1662
01:12:52,080 --> 01:12:53,680
that's just the residual connection.

1663
01:12:53,680 --> 01:12:58,420
So just think of it like this and I think it will be simpler.

1664
01:12:58,420 --> 01:13:02,680
And I definitely prefer this diagram instead.

1665
01:13:02,680 --> 01:13:05,380
But for language, I would just shift the input to the left

1666
01:13:05,380 --> 01:13:08,300
and do causal attention.

1667
01:13:08,300 --> 01:13:11,380
Last slide is going to be just showing now how

1668
01:13:11,380 --> 01:13:14,020
I can put together some language modeling and image modeling

1669
01:13:14,020 --> 01:13:16,440
to do more complicated things.

1670
01:13:16,440 --> 01:13:18,820
So here is a case where I will take

1671
01:13:18,820 --> 01:13:20,900
my input, which is an image.

1672
01:13:20,900 --> 01:13:23,540
I'll tokenize it by mapping each of these patches

1673
01:13:23,540 --> 01:13:25,600
into token vectors.

1674
01:13:25,600 --> 01:13:28,200
I'll do self-attention and some computer vision stuff here,

1675
01:13:28,200 --> 01:13:31,035
but it's all generic processing of tokens now.

1676
01:13:31,035 --> 01:13:33,160
And then I'll do this thing called cross-attention.

1677
01:13:33,160 --> 01:13:36,920
So now I'm going to try to be describing this image in text.

1678
01:13:36,920 --> 01:13:39,160
The task is to take an image and turn it into text.

1679
01:13:39,160 --> 01:13:40,820
Write a caption for the image.

1680
01:13:40,820 --> 01:13:43,740
So I'll take this as input.

1681
01:13:43,740 --> 01:13:45,860
And then I will autoregressively start

1682
01:13:45,860 --> 01:13:49,260
writing a sentence where every next step in my prediction

1683
01:13:49,260 --> 01:13:52,140
problem will be capable of attending

1684
01:13:52,140 --> 01:13:54,460
to the previous words in my sentence,

1685
01:13:54,460 --> 01:13:58,260
and additionally all of the representations of the image

1686
01:13:58,260 --> 01:13:59,118
tokens.

1687
01:13:59,118 --> 01:14:00,660
And cross-attention is just referring

1688
01:14:00,660 --> 01:14:06,060
to when you are having these tokens attend

1689
01:14:06,060 --> 01:14:08,275
to these tokens, which are not themselves.

1690
01:14:08,275 --> 01:14:09,900
They're like a different set of tokens.

1691
01:14:09,900 --> 01:14:11,540
So you can have all different types

1692
01:14:11,540 --> 01:14:13,780
of attention-masking strategies.

1693
01:14:13,780 --> 01:14:17,580
And this one is popular for image-to-text captioning.

1694
01:14:17,580 --> 01:14:22,060
We'll come back to generative models and autoregressive models

1695
01:14:22,060 --> 01:14:23,420
a little bit later.

1696
01:14:23,420 --> 01:14:25,890
And I'll end there for today.
